chemAddQuestions([
["m1-a-001",1,1,1,"Concept","mcq","Which of the following is NOT an SI base unit?",["kelvin (K)","kilogram (kg)","liter (L)","mole (mol)","second (s)"],2,"The SI base units the course uses are the kilogram (mass), meter (length), second (time), kelvin (temperature) and mole (amount); ampere and candela complete the seven. The liter is a DERIVED unit of volume built from length: 1 L = 1 dm³ = 10⁻³ m³. Trap: the liter is used so often that it feels fundamental, but volume is length cubed.",["There are seven SI base units; volume is not one of the seven quantities.","Ask which unit can be written in terms of another unit.","1 L = 1 dm³ - it is built from the meter."]],
["m1-a-002",1,1,1,"Trap","mcq","What is the SI base unit of mass?",["gram","kilogram","pound","milligram","newton"],1,"The SI base unit of mass is the kilogram (kg) - the only base unit that already carries a prefix. The gram is a perfectly good unit, but it is 10⁻³ of the base unit. The pound is not SI, and the newton is the unit of FORCE (weight), not mass. Trap: \"gram\" looks like the root unit, but the base unit is the kilogram.",["One SI base unit is unusual because its name already contains a prefix.","Mass, not weight: the newton measures force.","kilo- is built into the base unit of mass."]],
["m1-a-003",1,1,1,"Concept","mcq","The prefix micro- (μ) means multiply the unit by:",["10⁻³","10⁻⁹","10⁻¹²","10⁻⁶","10⁶"],3,"micro (μ) = 10⁻⁶, so 1 μg = 10⁻⁶ g. For comparison: milli (m) = 10⁻³, nano (n) = 10⁻⁹, pico (p) = 10⁻¹², and mega (M) = 10⁶. Trap: 10⁶ is mega - the same size of exponent with the opposite sign.",["Order the small prefixes: milli, micro, nano, pico.","Each step down that list is a factor of 10⁻³.","milli is 10⁻³, so the next one down is 10⁻⁶."]],
["m1-a-004",1,1,1,"Concept","mcq","Which prefix corresponds to a factor of 10⁻⁹?",["nano- (n)","pico- (p)","micro- (μ)","milli- (m)","centi- (c)"],0,"nano (n) = 10⁻⁹ - the prefix of the nanometer used for wavelengths of light. pico = 10⁻¹², micro = 10⁻⁶, milli = 10⁻³, centi = 10⁻². Trap: pico and micro are the neighbours on either side.",["Visible light is about 400-700 of these meters.","milli 10⁻³ → micro 10⁻⁶ → ? 10⁻⁹.","It is the prefix in \"nm\"."]],
["m1-a-005",1,1,2,"Calculation","mcq","A red laser pointer emits light of wavelength 635 nm. What is this wavelength in meters?",["6.35 × 10⁻⁹ m","6.35 × 10⁻⁴ m","6.35 × 10¹¹ m","6.35 × 10⁻¹¹ m","6.35 × 10⁻⁷ m"],4,"635 nm × (10⁻⁹ m / 1 nm) = 635 × 10⁻⁹ m = 6.35 × 10⁻⁷ m. Moving the decimal two places left (635 → 6.35) raises the exponent by 2 (10⁻⁹ → 10⁻⁷). Distractors: 6.35 × 10⁻⁹ m forgets that 635 = 6.35 × 10²; 6.35 × 10⁻⁴ m uses micro (10⁻⁶) instead of nano; 6.35 × 10¹¹ m multiplies by 10⁹ instead of 10⁻⁹; 6.35 × 10⁻¹¹ m shifts the exponent the wrong way.",["nano means 10⁻⁹.","Write 635 × 10⁻⁹ m, then put 635 into proper scientific notation.","635 = 6.35 × 10², so the exponent becomes −9 + 2."]],
["m1-a-006",1,1,2,"Calculation","mcq","A radio station broadcasts at 96.5 MHz. What is this frequency in hertz (s⁻¹)?",["9.65 × 10⁴ Hz","9.65 × 10¹⁰ Hz","9.65 × 10⁶ Hz","9.65 × 10⁻⁵ Hz","9.65 × 10⁷ Hz"],4,"M (mega) = 10⁶: 96.5 MHz = 96.5 × 10⁶ Hz = 9.65 × 10⁷ Hz. Distractors: 9.65 × 10⁴ Hz treats M as kilo; 9.65 × 10¹⁰ Hz treats it as giga; 9.65 × 10⁶ Hz forgets that 96.5 = 9.65 × 10¹; 9.65 × 10⁻⁵ Hz divides by 10⁶ (confusing M with μ).",["Capital M is mega.","mega = 10⁶; write 96.5 × 10⁶ Hz.","Shift 96.5 to 9.65 and add 1 to the exponent."]],
["m1-a-007",1,1,2,"Calculation","mcq","Express 0.0250 mg in micrograms (μg).",["0.0000250 μg","2.50 × 10⁴ μg","2.50 μg","25.0 μg","25 μg"],3,"1 mg = 10⁻³ g and 1 μg = 10⁻⁶ g, so 1 mg = 1000 μg. 0.0250 mg × (1000 μg / 1 mg) = 25.0 μg. The measurement 0.0250 has three significant figures (the trailing zero after the decimal point counts), so the answer is 25.0 μg, not 25 μg. Distractors: 0.0000250 μg divides by 1000 (wrong direction); 2.50 × 10⁴ μg multiplies by 10⁶ (forgot the milli); 2.50 μg uses a factor of 100; 25 μg drops a significant zero.",["A microgram is smaller than a milligram, so the number must get bigger.","1 mg = 1000 μg.","0.0250 × 1000, keeping three significant figures."]],
["m1-a-008",1,1,2,"Calculation","num","Convert 0.0458 km to centimeters. (Enter a number in cm.)",["4.58 × 10³ cm"],4580,"0.0458 km × (1000 m / 1 km) × (100 cm / 1 m) = 0.0458 × 10⁵ cm = 4.58 × 10³ cm (4580 cm, three significant figures). Check: a kilometer is 10⁵ cm, so the number must grow by 10⁵.",["Go km → m → cm.","1 km = 10³ m and 1 m = 10² cm, so 1 km = 10⁵ cm.","0.0458 × 10⁵ = ?"]],
["m1-a-009",1,1,3,"Calculation","mcq","A sample of a hormone has a mass of 7.8 ng. What is this mass in milligrams?",["7.8 × 10⁻³ mg","7.8 × 10⁻⁹ mg","7.8 × 10⁻⁶ mg","7.8 × 10⁶ mg","7.8 × 10³ mg"],2,"7.8 ng × (10⁻⁹ g / 1 ng) × (1 mg / 10⁻³ g) = 7.8 × 10⁻⁶ mg. A nanogram is a millionth of a milligram (10⁻⁹ / 10⁻³ = 10⁻⁶). Distractors: 7.8 × 10⁻³ mg goes only one prefix step (ng → μg); 7.8 × 10⁻⁹ mg stops at grams and relabels them as mg; 7.8 × 10⁶ mg and 7.8 × 10³ mg move in the wrong direction - a smaller unit to a larger one must give a SMALLER number.",["Is a milligram bigger or smaller than a nanogram? So should the number shrink or grow?","Convert ng → g, then g → mg.","10⁻⁹ g per ng divided by 10⁻³ g per mg gives 10⁻⁶."]],
["m1-a-010",1,1,3,"Concept","mcq","Arrange these lengths in order of INCREASING size: 2.5 × 10⁷ nm, 0.0030 km, 3.5 × 10² mm, 25 cm.",["0.0030 km < 25 cm < 3.5 × 10² mm < 2.5 × 10⁷ nm","2.5 × 10⁷ nm < 25 cm < 3.5 × 10² mm < 0.0030 km","0.0030 km < 3.5 × 10² mm < 25 cm < 2.5 × 10⁷ nm","25 cm < 2.5 × 10⁷ nm < 3.5 × 10² mm < 0.0030 km","2.5 × 10⁷ nm < 3.5 × 10² mm < 25 cm < 0.0030 km"],1,"Convert everything to meters: 2.5 × 10⁷ nm × 10⁻⁹ = 0.025 m; 25 cm = 0.25 m; 3.5 × 10² mm = 350 mm × 10⁻³ = 0.35 m; 0.0030 km = 3.0 m. Increasing: 0.025 m < 0.25 m < 0.35 m < 3.0 m. Distractors: ordering by the bare numbers (0.0030 looks smallest, 2.5 × 10⁷ biggest) ignores the units; the others mis-size the mm or nm conversion.",["You cannot compare numbers with different units - convert to one unit first.","Use meters: nm × 10⁻⁹, mm × 10⁻³, cm × 10⁻², km × 10³.","The values are 0.025, 3.0, 0.35 and 0.25 m."]],
["m1-a-011",1,1,1,"Concept","mcq","Exactly how large is 1 mL?",["1 cm³","1 dm³","10 cm³","1 × 10⁻³ cm³","100 mm³"],0,"By definition 1 L = 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³, so 1 mL = 10⁻³ L = 1 cm³ exactly. Distractors: 1 dm³ is a whole liter; 10 cm³ and 10⁻³ cm³ are off by factors of 10 and 1000; 1 cm³ = (10 mm)³ = 1000 mm³, so 100 mm³ is only a tenth of a mL.",["A liter is a cube 10 cm on each side.","1 L = 1000 cm³ and 1 L = 1000 mL.","So one mL and one cm³ are the same volume."]],
["m1-a-012",1,1,2,"Trap","mcq","How many cubic centimeters are in 1 m³?",["10² cm³","10³ cm³","10⁴ cm³","10⁹ cm³","10⁶ cm³"],4,"1 m = 100 cm, so 1 m³ = (100 cm)³ = 100³ cm³ = 10⁶ cm³. Both the number and the unit get cubed. Distractors: 10² forgets to cube the factor; 10⁴ squares it (that is cm² per m²); 10³ is the number of liters in a m³; 10⁹ is the number of mm³ in a m³.",["Start from the length relationship 1 m = 100 cm.","Cube both sides: (1 m)³ = (100 cm)³.","100³ = 1,000,000."]],
["m1-a-013",1,1,3,"Calculation","mcq","A storage tank holds 2.50 m³ of water. What is this volume in liters?",["2.50 L","2.50 × 10⁶ L","2.50 × 10⁻³ L","2.50 × 10³ L","250. L"],3,"1 m³ = 10⁶ cm³ = 10⁶ mL = 10³ L (equivalently 1 m³ = (10 dm)³ = 1000 dm³ = 1000 L). 2.50 m³ × (10³ L / 1 m³) = 2.50 × 10³ L. Distractors: 2.50 L treats m³ as liters; 2.50 × 10⁶ L stops at cm³ (mL) and calls them liters; 2.50 × 10⁻³ L divides instead of multiplying; 250. L uses a factor of 100 instead of 1000.",["A cubic meter is a big box; it holds much more than one liter.","1 m = 10 dm, so 1 m³ = 10³ dm³ - and 1 dm³ = 1 L.","Multiply by 1000 L per m³."]],
["m1-a-014",1,1,3,"Trap","mcq","A laboratory bench top has an area of 4.70 × 10⁴ cm². What is the area in m²?",["470. m²","0.0470 m²","4.70 m²","4.70 × 10⁸ m²","4.70 × 10⁶ m²"],2,"1 m = 100 cm, so 1 m² = (100 cm)² = 10⁴ cm². 4.70 × 10⁴ cm² × (1 m² / 10⁴ cm²) = 4.70 m². Distractors: 470. m² divides by 100 (forgot to square the factor); 0.0470 m² divides by 10⁶ (cubed it); 4.70 × 10⁸ m² and 4.70 × 10⁶ m² multiply instead of divide.",["Area units carry the conversion factor SQUARED.","1 m² = (100 cm)² = 10⁴ cm².","Divide 4.70 × 10⁴ by 10⁴."]],
["m1-a-015",1,1,3,"Calculation","mcq","A microscope slide has an area of 18.5 in². What is its area in cm²? (1 in = 2.54 cm exactly)",["119 cm²","47.0 cm²","7.28 cm²","2.87 cm²","303 cm²"],0,"1 in² = (2.54 cm)² = 6.4516 cm² (exact). 18.5 in² × 6.4516 cm²/in² = 119.35 cm² → 119 cm² (three significant figures from 18.5; the 2.54 is exact). Distractors: 47.0 cm² uses 2.54 only once (unit squared, number not); 7.28 and 2.87 cm² divide instead of multiply; 303 cm² cubes the factor.",["Square inches to square centimeters needs the factor squared.","(2.54 cm/in)² = 6.4516 cm²/in².","18.5 × 6.4516 = ?"]],
["m1-a-016",1,1,3,"Calculation","num","A rectangular aquarium measures 1.20 m × 0.500 m × 0.350 m. How many liters of water does it hold when full? (Enter a number in L.)",["2.10 × 10² L"],210,"V = 1.20 m × 0.500 m × 0.350 m = 0.210 m³. Since 1 m³ = 1000 L, V = 0.210 × 1000 = 2.10 × 10² L (210. L, three significant figures). Trap: 0.210 L comes from forgetting that a cubic meter is 1000 liters.",["Volume of a box = length × width × height.","The result is in m³; 1 m³ = 1000 L.","0.210 m³ × 1000 L/m³."]],
["m1-a-017",1,1,4,"Trap","mcq","Ethanol has a density of 0.789 g/cm³. What is its density in kg/m³?",["0.0789 kg/m³","789 kg/m³","7.89 × 10⁻⁴ kg/m³","7.89 × 10⁵ kg/m³","0.789 kg/m³"],1,"0.789 g/cm³ × (1 kg / 1000 g) × (10⁶ cm³ / 1 m³) = 0.789 × 10³ kg/m³ = 789 kg/m³. The cm³ → m³ factor is (100)³ = 10⁶ and it goes in the numerator because cm³ is in the denominator of the starting unit. Distractors: 0.0789 kg/m³ uses 100 cm³ per m³ (did not cube); 7.89 × 10⁻⁴ kg/m³ flips the volume factor; 7.89 × 10⁵ kg/m³ forgets g → kg; 0.789 kg/m³ assumes the number does not change.",["Convert the numerator (g → kg) and the denominator (cm³ → m³) separately.","There are 10⁶ cm³ in a m³; a \"per cm³\" quantity becomes 10⁶ times bigger per m³.","0.789 ÷ 1000 × 10⁶."]],
["m1-a-018",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","Which of the following equalities is FALSE?",["1 L = 1 dm³","1 L = 100 cm³","1 mL = 1 cm³","1 m³ = 1000 L","1 cm³ = 10⁻⁶ m³"],1,"1 L = 1 dm³ = (10 cm)³ = 1000 cm³, so \"1 L = 100 cm³\" is false. The others are all true: 1 mL = 1 cm³; 1 m³ = (10 dm)³ = 1000 dm³ = 1000 L; 1 cm³ = (10⁻² m)³ = 10⁻⁶ m³.",["Check each line by cubing a length relationship.","1 dm = 10 cm, so 1 dm³ = ? cm³.","A liter is 1000 cm³, not 100."]],
["m1-a-019",1,1,2,"Calculation","num","A gas sample has a volume of 1.20 × 10⁴ cm³. What is its volume in cubic meters? (Enter a number in m³.)",["0.0120 m³"],0.012,"1 m³ = (100 cm)³ = 10⁶ cm³, so 1.20 × 10⁴ cm³ × (1 m³ / 10⁶ cm³) = 1.20 × 10⁻² m³ = 0.0120 m³. Trap: dividing by 100 (not cubing the factor) gives 120 m³, a volume the size of a large room.",["Cubic units need the length factor cubed.","1 m³ = 10⁶ cm³.","Divide 1.20 × 10⁴ by 10⁶."]],
["m1-a-020",1,1,2,"Calculation","mcq","Dry ice (solid CO₂) sublimes at −78 °C. What is this temperature in kelvins?",["351 K","−195 K","195 K","−351 K","195.15 K"],2,"T(K) = T(°C) + 273.15 = −78 + 273.15 = 195.15 → 195 K. In addition, keep the fewest decimal places: −78 has none, so the answer is rounded to the ones place. Distractors: 351 K adds 273 to +78 (dropped the minus sign); −195 K and −351 K are impossible (no negative kelvin temperatures); 195.15 K reports more precision than −78 °C has.",["K = °C + 273.15.","Keep the sign of −78 °C.","Addition rule: −78 has no decimal places, so round 195.15 to the ones place."]],
["m1-a-021",1,1,2,"Calculation","mcq","Room temperature is 22.5 °C. What is it in °F?",["72.5 °F","40.5 °F","44.5 °F","−5.3 °F","98.1 °F"],0,"°F = (9/5)(°C) + 32 = (9/5)(22.5) + 32 = 40.5 + 32 = 72.5 °F. Distractors: 40.5 °F forgets to add 32; 44.5 °F uses 5/9 instead of 9/5; −5.3 °F uses the °F → °C formula backwards; 98.1 °F adds 32 BEFORE multiplying by 9/5.",["Use °F = (9/5)°C + 32.","Change the degree size first (× 9/5), then shift the zero (+ 32).","9/5 × 22.5 = 40.5."]],
["m1-a-022",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","At what temperature do the Celsius and Fahrenheit scales show the same number?",["0°","32°","−32°","−40°","−273.15°"],3,"Set °F = °C = x in °F = (9/5)°C + 32: x = (9/5)x + 32 → −(4/5)x = 32 → x = −40. So −40 °C = −40 °F. Distractors: 0 °C is 32 °F and 32 °F is 0 °C (the freezing point of water on each scale); −273.15 °C is absolute zero, which is −459.67 °F.",["Call the shared reading x and substitute it into °F = (9/5)°C + 32.","x = 1.8x + 32.","Solve: −0.8x = 32."]],
["m1-a-023",1,1,3,"Calculation","num","Paper ignites at about 451 °F. Express this temperature in kelvins. (Enter a number in K.)",["506 K"],506,"°C = (451 − 32) × 5/9 = 419 × 5/9 = 232.78 °C; K = 232.78 + 273.15 = 505.93 → 506 K. Trap: forgetting to subtract 32 first gives 524 K; skipping the Celsius step altogether (451 + 273) gives 724 K.",["There is no direct °F → K formula; go through °C.","°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9.","Then add 273.15 to the Celsius value."]],
["m1-a-024",1,1,1,"Concept","mcq","What is absolute zero on the Celsius scale?",["0 °C","−459.67 °C","273.15 °C","−32 °C","−273.15 °C"],4,"Absolute zero is 0 K, and T(°C) = T(K) − 273.15, so absolute zero = −273.15 °C. Distractors: 0 °C is the freezing point of water; −459.67 is absolute zero on the FAHRENHEIT scale; +273.15 has the wrong sign; −32 confuses the 32 °F freezing point.",["Absolute zero is 0 on the kelvin scale.","K = °C + 273.15, so °C = K − 273.15.","0 − 273.15 = ?"]],
["m1-a-025",1,1,2,"Trap","mcq","During a reaction the temperature of a solution rises by 15.0 °C. What is the temperature CHANGE in kelvins?",["288.2 K","27.0 K","8.33 K","15.0 K","−258.2 K"],3,"A kelvin and a Celsius degree are the same size; the scales differ only in where zero is. A change of 15.0 °C is therefore a change of 15.0 K. Adding 273.15 is only for converting an actual temperature, not a difference. Distractors: 288.2 K adds 273.15 to a difference; 27.0 K multiplies by 9/5 (that is the change in °F); 8.33 K multiplies by 5/9; −258.2 K subtracts 273.15.",["Is this an absolute temperature or a difference between two temperatures?","ΔT(K) = T₂(K) − T₁(K) = (T₂ + 273.15) − (T₁ + 273.15).","The 273.15 cancels."]],
["m1-a-026",1,1,4,"Exam","mcq","Which of the following temperatures is the LOWEST?",["−50 °F","−40 °C","230 K","−42 °C","0 °F"],0,"Convert all to kelvins. −50 °F: (−50 − 32) × 5/9 = −45.56 °C = 227.6 K. −40 °C = 233.15 K. 230 K. −42 °C = 231.15 K. 0 °F: (0 − 32) × 5/9 = −17.78 °C = 255.4 K. The lowest is −50 °F (about 227.6 K). Trap: comparing the raw numbers (−50 vs −42 vs −40) only works on the same scale.",["Put every temperature on one scale before comparing.","Kelvin is easiest: °F → °C → K.","−50 °F is about −45.6 °C."]],
["m1-a-027",1,1,3,"Calculation","mcq","Liquid nitrogen boils at 77 K. What is this temperature in °F?",["−353 °F","−77 °F","−321 °F","−295 °F","171 °F"],2,"°C = 77 − 273.15 = −196.15 °C. °F = (9/5)(−196.15) + 32 = −353.07 + 32 = −321.07 → −321 °F. Distractors: −353 °F forgets the +32; −77 °F uses 5/9; −295 °F adds 32 before multiplying; 171 °F treats 77 K as 77 °C.",["Two steps: K → °C, then °C → °F.","°C = K − 273.15 = −196.15.","°F = 1.8 × (−196.15) + 32."]],
["m1-a-028",1,1,4,"Trap","mcq","At what Kelvin temperature is the Fahrenheit reading exactly twice the Celsius reading?",["160 K","433 K","320 K","593 K","249 K"],1,"Let the Celsius reading be C. Then °F = 2C, and °F = (9/5)C + 32, so 2C = 1.8C + 32 → 0.2C = 32 → C = 160 °C (and °F = 320 °F). In kelvins: 160 + 273.15 = 433.15 ≈ 433 K. Distractors: 160 K stops at the Celsius value; 320 K reports the Fahrenheit value; 593 K adds 273 to the Fahrenheit value; 249 K solves the reversed condition C = 2F.",["Write °F = 2C and substitute into the conversion formula.","2C = 1.8C + 32.","C = 160 °C; the question wants kelvins."]],
["m1-a-029",1,1,4,"Trap","mcq","Two temperatures differ by 18.0 Fahrenheit degrees. By how many kelvins do they differ?",["265.4 K","32.4 K","−7.8 K","291.2 K","10.0 K"],4,"A Fahrenheit degree is 5/9 the size of a Celsius degree (180 F° span the same range as 100 C°), and a Celsius degree equals a kelvin. ΔT = 18.0 F° × (5 K / 9 F°) = 10.0 K. No 32 and no 273.15 appear, because those shift ZERO points and cancel in a difference. Distractors: 265.4 K converts 18.0 °F as if it were an actual temperature; 32.4 K multiplies by 9/5; −7.8 K subtracts 32 from a difference; 291.2 K adds 273.15 to 18.0.",["This is a temperature DIFFERENCE, not a temperature.","Only the size of the degree matters: 9 F° = 5 C° = 5 K.","18.0 × 5/9."]],
["m1-a-030",1,1,2,"Calculation","num","Normal body temperature is 98.6 °F. What is it in °C? (Enter a number in °C.)",["37.0 °C"],37,"°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9 = (98.6 − 32) × 5/9 = 66.6 × 5/9 = 37.0 °C. Subtract 32 FIRST (shift the zero), then change the degree size. Trap: 98.6 × 5/9 − 32 = 22.8 gives a nonsense body temperature.",["Undo the °F formula in reverse order.","Subtract 32, then multiply by 5/9.","66.6 × 5/9 = ?"]],
["m1-a-031",1,1,2,"Calculation","mcq","A 25.0 g piece of metal has a volume of 9.26 cm³. What is its density, correctly rounded?",["0.370 g/cm³","232 g/cm³","2.7 g/cm³","2.6998 g/cm³","2.70 g/cm³"],4,"d = m/V = 25.0 g / 9.26 cm³ = 2.6998 g/cm³ → 2.70 g/cm³ (both data have three significant figures, so the answer has three - the trailing zero is significant). Distractors: 0.370 g/cm³ is V/m; 232 g/cm³ multiplies; 2.7 g/cm³ drops a significant zero; 2.6998 g/cm³ has too many digits.",["Density = mass ÷ volume.","Divide, then apply the multiplication/division sig-fig rule.","Three significant figures: 2.6998 rounds to ?"]],
["m1-a-032",1,1,2,"Calculation","num","An 8.50 g sample of a liquid occupies 10.2 mL. What is its density? (Enter a number in g/mL.)",["0.833 g/mL"],0.833,"d = m/V = 8.50 g ÷ 10.2 mL = 0.83333 → 0.833 g/mL (three significant figures). It is less dense than water. Trap: V/m = 1.20 mL/g has the wrong units for a density.",["Density = mass ÷ volume.","Keep three significant figures.","8.50 g divided by 10.2 mL."]],
["m1-a-033",1,1,2,"Calculation","mcq","Gold has a density of 19.3 g/cm³. What is the mass of a 12.0 cm³ gold bar?",["0.622 g","1.61 g","231.6 g","232 g","31.3 g"],3,"mass = density × volume = 19.3 g/cm³ × 12.0 cm³ = 231.6 g → 232 g (three significant figures). Distractors: 0.622 g is V/d and 1.61 g is d/V (the units would not even cancel to grams); 231.6 g has too many significant figures; 31.3 g adds the numbers.",["Use density as a conversion factor: 19.3 g = 1 cm³.","cm³ × (g/cm³) leaves grams.","19.3 × 12.0, rounded to three significant figures."]],
["m1-a-034",1,1,2,"Calculation","mcq","Ethanol has a density of 0.789 g/mL. What volume does 250.0 g of ethanol occupy?",["197 mL","317 mL","3.16 × 10⁻³ mL","316.86 mL","0.317 mL"],1,"V = m/d = 250.0 g ÷ 0.789 g/mL = 316.86 mL → 317 mL (0.789 has only three significant figures). Check: ethanol is less dense than water, so 250 g takes up MORE than 250 mL. Distractors: 197 mL multiplies (units would be g²/mL); 3.16 × 10⁻³ mL is d/m; 316.86 mL has too many digits; 0.317 mL divides by 1000 as if converting to liters but keeps mL.",["Rearrange d = m/V for V.","V = m ÷ d.","Less dense than water, so the volume in mL should exceed the mass in g."]],
["m1-a-035",1,1,4,"Trap","mcq","A graduated cylinder contains 19.9 mL of water. When a 34.7 g piece of metal is added, the water level rises to 24.5 mL. What is the density of the metal, correctly rounded?",["7.54 g/mL","1.42 g/mL","7.5 g/mL","1.74 g/mL","0.13 g/mL"],2,"Volume of metal = volume displaced = 24.5 mL − 19.9 mL = 4.6 mL. Subtraction keeps the fewest DECIMAL places (one), so the volume has only TWO significant figures. d = 34.7 g / 4.6 mL = 7.5435 → 7.5 g/mL (two significant figures). Distractors: 7.54 g/mL keeps three significant figures; 1.42 and 1.74 g/mL divide by the total water level instead of the change; 0.13 g/mL is V/m.",["The metal's volume is the CHANGE in the water level.","After subtracting, count the significant figures of the difference, not of the readings.","4.6 mL has 2 significant figures, so the density does too."]],
["m1-a-036",1,1,3,"Calculation","num","An aluminum cube (density 2.70 g/cm³) has an edge length of 2.50 cm. What is its mass? (Enter a number in g.)",["42.2 g"],42.2,"V = (2.50 cm)³ = 15.625 cm³; m = d × V = 2.70 g/cm³ × 15.625 cm³ = 42.1875 g → 42.2 g. Trap: using the edge length (or its square) as the volume gives 6.75 g or 16.9 g.",["First find the volume of the cube.","V = edge³.","Mass = density × volume."]],
["m1-a-037",1,1,4,"Trap","mcq","A lead sphere (density 11.3 g/cm³) has a DIAMETER of 1.20 cm. What is its mass? (V of a sphere = (4/3)πr³)",["10.2 g","81.8 g","7.67 g","17.0 g","0.0801 g"],0,"r = diameter/2 = 0.600 cm. V = (4/3)π(0.600 cm)³ = 0.9048 cm³. m = 11.3 g/cm³ × 0.9048 cm³ = 10.22 → 10.2 g. Distractors: 81.8 g uses the diameter as the radius (8× too big); 7.67 g drops the 4/3; 17.0 g squares the radius; 0.0801 g divides V by d.",["The formula needs the RADIUS.","r = 0.600 cm; cube it before multiplying.","m = 11.3 × (4/3)π(0.600)³."]],
["m1-a-038",1,1,3,"Concept","mcq","Water has a density of 1.00 g/mL. Which object will FLOAT in water?",["a block with mass 50.0 g and volume 40.0 cm³","a block with mass 100. g and volume 95.0 cm³","a block with mass 8.0 g and volume 10.0 cm³","a block with mass 3.0 g and volume 2.5 cm³","a block with mass 27.0 g and volume 10.0 cm³"],2,"An object floats if its density is LESS than the liquid's. Densities: 8.0/10.0 = 0.80 g/cm³ (floats); 50.0/40.0 = 1.25; 100./95.0 = 1.05; 3.0/2.5 = 1.2; 27.0/10.0 = 2.70 g/cm³ (all sink). Trap: the lightest object is not automatically the one that floats - the 3.0 g block is denser than water.",["Compare densities, not masses.","Compute m/V for each block.","Floats if d is below 1.00 g/cm³."]],
["m1-a-039",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","Equal 100-g masses of gold (19.3 g/cm³), lead (11.3 g/cm³), aluminum (2.70 g/cm³), water (1.00 g/mL) and ethanol (0.789 g/mL) are measured. Which sample occupies the GREATEST volume?",["gold","ethanol","lead","aluminum","water"],1,"V = m/d. With the same mass, the smallest density gives the largest volume: ethanol, 100 g / 0.789 g/mL = 127 mL (gold only 5.18 cm³). Trap: picking gold confuses \"densest\" with \"largest\".",["Volume = mass ÷ density.","Same mass in every case, so only the density changes.","Smallest density → largest volume."]],
["m1-a-040",1,1,3,"Calculation","mcq","An empty flask has a mass of 45.27 g. Filled with 50.0 mL of a liquid, its mass is 92.84 g. What is the density of the liquid?",["1.86 g/mL","0.905 g/mL","1.05 g/mL","0.951 g/mL","0.9514 g/mL"],3,"Mass of liquid = 92.84 g − 45.27 g = 47.57 g (four significant figures - subtraction keeps two decimal places). d = 47.57 g / 50.0 mL = 0.95140 → 0.951 g/mL (50.0 limits it to three). Distractors: 1.86 g/mL uses the full flask mass; 0.905 g/mL uses the empty flask; 1.05 g/mL is V/m; 0.9514 g/mL keeps a fourth significant figure.",["First find the mass of the liquid alone.","Mass by difference, then d = m/V.","47.57 g ÷ 50.0 mL, three significant figures."]],
["m1-a-041",1,1,4,"Exam","mcq","A \"gold\" ring with a mass of 5.25 g raises the water level in a graduated cylinder by 0.40 mL. Pure gold has a density of 19.3 g/cm³. Which conclusion is correct?",["d = 13 g/mL, well below 19.3 g/cm³, so the ring is not pure gold","d = 13.1 g/mL, close enough to call the ring pure gold","d = 0.076 g/mL, so the ring must be hollow","d = 2.1 g/mL, so the ring is mostly aluminum","d = 13 g/mL, which matches gold because density depends on sample size"],0,"Volume displaced = 0.40 mL = 0.40 cm³ (two significant figures). d = 5.25 g / 0.40 cm³ = 13.125 → 13 g/cm³. That is far below gold's 19.3 g/cm³, so the ring contains a less dense metal. Distractors: 13.1 has too many significant figures AND the wrong conclusion; 0.076 is V/m; 2.1 multiplies m × V; density is INTENSIVE, so it does not depend on sample size.",["Density from mass and displaced volume.","Two significant figures from 0.40 mL.","Compare 13 with 19.3 - is it the same material?"]],
["m1-a-042",1,1,3,"Calculation","num","What volume is occupied by 1.00 kg of an aqueous salt solution whose density is 1.19 g/mL? (Enter a number in mL.)",["840 mL"],840,"1.00 kg = 1.00 × 10³ g. V = m/d = 1.00 × 10³ g ÷ 1.19 g/mL = 840.34 → 840. mL (three significant figures). Trap: forgetting kg → g gives 0.840 mL.",["Get the mass in grams to match the density units.","V = m ÷ d.","1000 g ÷ 1.19 g/mL."]],
["m1-a-043",1,1,3,"Calculation","mcq","Mercury has a density of 13.6 g/mL. What is the mass of 2.50 L of mercury?",["34.0 g","0.184 kg","3.40 × 10⁷ kg","5.44 kg","34.0 kg"],4,"2.50 L = 2.50 × 10³ mL. m = 2.50 × 10³ mL × 13.6 g/mL = 3.40 × 10⁴ g = 34.0 kg. Distractors: 34.0 g never converts L to mL; 0.184 kg is V/d; 3.40 × 10⁷ kg multiplies by 1000 when going g → kg; 5.44 kg is d/V.",["The density is per mL, so convert the volume to mL first.","m = V × d gives grams.","3.40 × 10⁴ g ÷ 1000 g/kg."]],
["m1-a-044",1,1,4,"Exam","mcq","A rectangular slab of a soft metal measures 15.0 mm × 12.5 mm × 8.00 mm and has a mass of 3.24 × 10³ mg. What is its density in g/cm³?",["2.16 × 10³ g/cm³","2.16 × 10⁻³ g/cm³","2.16 g/cm³","0.216 g/cm³","0.463 g/cm³"],2,"Mass: 3.24 × 10³ mg × (1 g / 1000 mg) = 3.24 g. Edges in cm: 1.50, 1.25 and 0.800 cm, so V = 1.50 × 1.25 × 0.800 = 1.50 cm³. d = 3.24 g / 1.50 cm³ = 2.16 g/cm³. Distractors: 2.16 × 10³ leaves the mass in mg; 2.16 × 10⁻³ leaves the volume in mm³; 0.216 converts only two of the three edges; 0.463 is V/m.",["Convert mass to g and every edge to cm before combining.","10 mm = 1 cm for EACH of the three edges.","V = 1.50 × 1.25 × 0.800 cm³."]],
["m1-a-045",1,1,4,"Calculation","mcq","A rectangular sheet of aluminum foil (density 2.70 g/cm³) measures 25.0 cm × 30.5 cm and has a mass of 1.85 g. How thick is the foil in micrometers (μm)?",["899 μm","8.99 × 10⁻⁴ μm","65.5 μm","8.99 μm","0.899 μm"],3,"V = m/d = 1.85 g ÷ 2.70 g/cm³ = 0.6852 cm³. Area = 25.0 × 30.5 = 762.5 cm². Thickness = V/area = 8.986 × 10⁻⁴ cm. 1 cm = 10⁴ μm, so thickness = 8.99 μm. Distractors: 899 μm uses 10⁶ (that is m → μm, not cm → μm); 8.99 × 10⁻⁴ μm never converts from cm; 65.5 μm multiplies m × d; 0.899 μm uses 10³.",["Volume from mass and density; thickness = volume ÷ area.","The thickness comes out in cm.","1 cm = 10⁻² m = 10⁴ μm."]],
["m1-a-046",1,1,4,"Trap","mcq","The density of air at 20 °C is 1.20 g/L. What is this density in kg/m³?",["1.20 × 10⁻³ kg/m³","1.20 × 10³ kg/m³","1.20 × 10⁻⁶ kg/m³","1.20 × 10⁶ kg/m³","1.20 kg/m³"],4,"1.20 g/L × (1 kg / 1000 g) × (1000 L / 1 m³) = 1.20 kg/m³. The two factors of 1000 cancel: g/L and kg/m³ are numerically identical units. Distractors come from applying only one of the two factors (1.20 × 10⁻³ or 1.20 × 10³) or using 10⁶ for one of them.",["Convert numerator and denominator separately.","g → kg divides by 1000; per L → per m³ multiplies by 1000 (1 m³ = 1000 L).","What happens when you multiply and divide by the same factor?"]],
["m1-a-047",1,1,4,"Exam","mcq","A 15.32 g piece of metal is lowered into an overflow can full of water, and 5.67 g of water (density 1.00 g/mL) spills out. What is the density of the metal?",["2.70 g/mL","0.370 g/mL","86.9 g/mL","2.702 g/mL","1.70 g/mL"],0,"The metal displaces its own volume of water: V = 5.67 g ÷ 1.00 g/mL = 5.67 mL. d = 15.32 g / 5.67 mL = 2.7019 → 2.70 g/mL (5.67 and 1.00 both have three significant figures). Distractors: 0.370 is V/m; 86.9 multiplies; 2.702 has four significant figures; 1.70 subtracts the water mass from the metal mass.",["The spilled water has the same volume as the metal.","Turn the mass of water into a volume with water's density.","d = 15.32 g ÷ 5.67 mL."]],
["m1-a-048",1,1,3,"Calculation","mcq","Which sample has the GREATEST mass?",["10.0 kg of water","1.00 L of mercury (13.6 g/mL)","5.00 L of ethanol (0.789 g/mL)","500. cm³ of gold (19.3 g/cm³)","12.0 L of air (1.20 g/L)"],1,"Masses: mercury 1000 mL × 13.6 g/mL = 13.6 kg; water 10.0 kg; ethanol 5000 mL × 0.789 = 3.95 kg; gold 500. × 19.3 = 9.65 kg; air 12.0 × 1.20 = 14.4 g = 0.0144 kg. Mercury wins. Trap: the largest volume (air) is the lightest by far.",["Convert every sample to a mass in the same unit.","m = V × d; watch L vs mL.","Mercury comes to 13.6 kg."]],
["m1-a-049",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","As the temperature of most liquids increases, their density:",["increases, because the molecules move faster","stays the same, because density is an intensive property","decreases, because the mass decreases","decreases, because the volume increases while the mass stays the same","increases, because the volume decreases"],3,"Most substances expand when heated: the same mass occupies more volume, so d = m/V falls. (Silberberg notes that because volume changes with temperature, so does density.) Distractors: being intensive means density does not depend on the AMOUNT of sample - it can still depend on temperature; heating does not change mass.",["Mass does not change on heating.","What happens to the volume of a liquid as it warms (think of a thermometer)?","Larger V with the same m gives a smaller m/V."]],
["m1-a-050",1,1,3,"Calculation","num","Lead has a density of 11.3 g/cm³. What volume does 1.00 lb of lead occupy? (1 kg = 2.205 lb; enter a number in cm³.)",["40.1 cm³"],40.1,"1.00 lb × (1 kg / 2.205 lb) × (1000 g / 1 kg) = 453.5 g. V = m/d = 453.5 g ÷ 11.3 g/cm³ = 40.13 → 40.1 cm³. Trap: multiplying by 2.205 instead of dividing gives 195 cm³.",["Convert pounds to grams first.","lb → kg (÷ 2.205), kg → g (× 1000).","Then V = m ÷ d."]],
["m1-a-051",1,1,4,"Exam","mcq","A metal cylinder is 5.08 cm long, has a diameter of 1.2 cm, and a mass of 15.32 g. What is its density, correctly rounded? (V = πr²h)",["2.67 g/cm³","2.7 g/cm³","0.667 g/cm³","8.4 g/cm³","0.38 g/cm³"],1,"r = 1.2/2 = 0.60 cm. V = π(0.60)²(5.08) = 5.745 cm³. d = 15.32 / 5.745 = 2.667 g/cm³. The diameter 1.2 cm has only TWO significant figures, so d = 2.7 g/cm³. Distractors: 2.67 keeps three significant figures; 0.667 uses the diameter as the radius; 8.4 drops π; 0.38 is V/m.",["Halve the diameter to get r.","The least precise measurement (1.2 cm) sets the sig figs.","Two significant figures: 2.666... becomes ?"]],
["m1-a-052",1,1,4,"Exam","mcq","A 0.250 kg piece of an unknown metal is dropped into a graduated cylinder, and the water level rises from 25.0 mL to 47.2 mL. Which metal is it? (Al 2.70, Fe 7.87, Cu 8.96, Ag 10.5, Pb 11.3 g/cm³)",["lead","silver","copper","iron","aluminum"],0,"V = 47.2 − 25.0 = 22.2 mL = 22.2 cm³. m = 0.250 kg = 250. g. d = 250. g / 22.2 cm³ = 11.3 g/cm³ → lead (11.3). Traps: using 25.0 mL as the volume gives 10.0 g/cm³ (looks like silver); using 47.2 mL gives 5.30; leaving the mass in kg gives 0.0113.",["Volume of the metal = the rise in water level.","Convert kg to g so the density is in g/cm³.","250. g ÷ 22.2 cm³ = ?"]],
["m1-a-053",1,1,4,"Exam","mcq","A copper wire (density 8.96 g/cm³) has a diameter of 0.0640 cm. What length of this wire, in meters, has a mass of 1.00 kg? (cross-section area = πr²)",["86.7 m","3.47 × 10⁴ m","0.347 m","1.09 × 10³ m","347 m"],4,"V = m/d = 1000 g ÷ 8.96 g/cm³ = 111.6 cm³. r = 0.0320 cm, area = π(0.0320)² = 3.217 × 10⁻³ cm². Length = V/area = 3.469 × 10⁴ cm = 347 m. Distractors: 86.7 m uses the diameter as the radius; 3.47 × 10⁴ m never converts cm to m; 0.347 m forgets kg → g; 1.09 × 10³ m drops π.",["A wire is a long cylinder: V = (area) × (length).","Get V from mass and density; get the area from the RADIUS.","length (cm) = V ÷ πr², then ÷ 100 for meters."]],
["m1-a-054",1,1,4,"Exam","mcq","An empty 250.0 mL volumetric flask has a mass of 87.236 g. Filled to the mark with a solution, its mass is 342.418 g. What is the density of the solution?",["1.0207 g/mL","1.370 g/mL","1.021 g/mL","0.9797 g/mL","1.02 g/mL"],2,"Mass of solution = 342.418 − 87.236 = 255.182 g (three decimal places, six significant figures). d = 255.182 g / 250.0 mL = 1.02073 → 1.021 g/mL. The volume 250.0 mL has FOUR significant figures, so the density has four. Distractors: 1.0207 keeps five; 1.370 uses the full flask mass; 0.9797 is V/m; 1.02 rounds to only three.",["Mass of solution by difference.","The flask volume 250.0 mL has four significant figures.","255.182 ÷ 250.0, rounded to four significant figures."]],
["m1-a-055",1,1,4,"Exam","mcq","A metal cube is 1.50 in on each edge and has a mass of 0.437 kg. What is its density in g/cm³? (1 in = 2.54 cm)",["7.90 g/cm³","51.0 g/cm³","0.00790 g/cm³","0.127 g/cm³","20.1 g/cm³"],0,"Edge = 1.50 in × 2.54 cm/in = 3.81 cm. V = (3.81 cm)³ = 55.306 cm³. m = 0.437 kg = 437 g. d = 437 / 55.306 = 7.901 → 7.90 g/cm³. Distractors: 51.0 converts in³ to cm³ with 2.54 only once; 20.1 squares it; 0.00790 leaves the mass in kg; 0.127 is V/m.",["Convert the edge length to cm BEFORE cubing (or cube the factor).","(2.54 cm/in)³ = 16.387 cm³/in³.","d = 437 g ÷ (3.81 cm)³."]],
["m1-a-056",1,1,3,"Calculation","num","Gasoline has a density of 0.737 g/mL. What is the mass of 1.00 gal of gasoline? (1 gal = 3.785 L; enter a number in kg.)",["2.79 kg"],2.79,"1.00 gal × 3.785 L/gal × 1000 mL/L = 3785 mL. m = 3785 mL × 0.737 g/mL = 2789.5 g = 2.79 kg. Trap: skipping L → mL gives 2.79 g, a thousand times too small.",["Chain gal → L → mL.","Then mass = volume × density.","Finally g → kg."]],
["m1-a-057",1,1,1,"Concept","mcq","Which of the following is an EXTENSIVE property?",["density","temperature","melting point","color","mass"],4,"Extensive properties depend on the amount of substance (mass, volume, heat content). Intensive properties do not (density, temperature, melting point, color). Cut a sample in half and its mass halves, but its density and melting point stay the same.",["Imagine doubling the sample. Which property doubles?","Extensive = depends on how much you have.","Mass and volume are the textbook examples."]],
["m1-a-058",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","Which pair consists of two INTENSIVE properties?",["mass and density","volume and temperature","mass and volume","density and boiling point","heat content and temperature"],3,"Density (a ratio of two extensive properties) and boiling point do not depend on the amount of sample, so both are intensive. Each distractor pairs one or two extensive properties (mass, volume, heat content) with an intensive one.",["Check each property separately: does it change if the sample doubles?","Mass, volume and heat are extensive.","Both members of the right pair stay the same for any sample size."]],
["m1-a-059",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","A copper block is cut exactly in half. Which property of EACH half is half of the original block's value?",["density","melting point","volume","temperature","color"],2,"Volume is extensive: each half has half the volume (and half the mass). Density = m/V stays the same because both m and V halve. Melting point, temperature and color are intensive.",["Which properties depend on the amount of copper?","Density is a ratio of two quantities that both halve.","Only an extensive property halves."]],
["m1-a-060",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","A bathtub of water and a cup of water are both at 40 °C. Which statement is correct?",["The bathtub is at a higher temperature because it contains more water","They are at the same temperature, but the bathtub contains much more heat (thermal energy)","Both contain the same amount of heat because their temperatures are equal","The cup is at a higher temperature because it is smaller","Temperature is extensive, so the bathtub's temperature is the sum of many cups"],1,"Temperature is intensive, so both are at 40 °C. The heat (thermal energy) content is extensive: the bathtub has far more water, so it holds far more energy - Silberberg's own example of heat vs temperature.",["Is temperature intensive or extensive?","Is heat content intensive or extensive?","Same temperature, very different amounts of energy."]],
["m1-a-061",1,1,1,"Concept","mcq","Which of the following is a PHYSICAL change?",["iron rusting","ice melting","wood burning","milk souring","an egg cooking"],1,"In a physical change the substance keeps its composition: ice and liquid water are both H₂O. Rusting, burning, souring and cooking all produce new substances with different compositions, so they are chemical changes.",["Ask: is a new substance formed?","A change of state is not a change in composition.","Melted ice is still H₂O."]],
["m1-a-062",1,1,1,"Concept","mcq","Which of the following is a CHEMICAL property?",["density","melting point","color","flammability","electrical conductivity"],3,"A chemical property describes how a substance changes into a different substance (here, whether it burns). Density, melting point, color and conductivity can be observed without changing the composition, so they are physical properties.",["Can you observe the property without turning the substance into something else?","Measuring conductivity leaves a wire unchanged.","Burning creates new substances."]],
["m1-a-063",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","Which process is a CHEMICAL change?",["sugar dissolving in water","water boiling","silver tarnishing","an aluminum can being crushed","an iron nail being magnetized"],2,"Tarnish is silver sulfide, a new substance formed from silver and sulfur compounds in air - a chemical change. Dissolving, boiling, crushing and magnetizing leave the substances' compositions unchanged.",["Look for a new substance with a new composition.","Tarnish is a different compound from silver metal.","Phase changes and changes of shape are physical."]],
["m1-a-064",1,1,3,"Concept","mcq","Consider these statements about copper: (1) it conducts electricity; (2) it reacts with nitric acid to give a brown gas; (3) it is reddish-brown; (4) it melts at 1085 °C; (5) it forms a green coating in moist air. Which are CHEMICAL properties?",["2 and 5 only","1, 2 and 5","2 only","3 and 5 only","1 and 4 only"],0,"Chemical properties involve forming new substances: reacting with nitric acid (2) and forming the green copper carbonate/hydroxide coating (5). Conductivity (1), color (3) and melting point (4) are observed without changing copper's composition, so they are physical.",["Mark each statement: does copper turn into something else?","Melting is a change of state, not of composition.","Two statements describe reactions."]],
["m1-a-065",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","Which statement correctly compares boiling water with passing an electric current through water to form H₂ and O₂?",["Both are physical changes","Both are chemical changes","Boiling is a chemical change; electrolysis is a physical change","Boiling breaks the O−H bonds, so it is a chemical change","Boiling is a physical change; electrolysis is a chemical change"],4,"Steam is still H₂O, so boiling only overcomes forces BETWEEN molecules - a physical change. Electrolysis turns water into two new substances, hydrogen and oxygen - a chemical change. Trap: boiling does not break covalent O−H bonds.",["What substance is present after each process?","Steam = H₂O; electrolysis products = H₂ and O₂.","A new substance means a chemical change."]],
["m1-a-066",1,1,3,"Concept","mcq","Which statement is FALSE?",["Frost forming on a cold window is a physical change","Burning gasoline is a physical change because the gasoline only changes from liquid to gas","A change that produces substances with new compositions is a chemical change","The temperature at which a substance melts is a physical property","A chemical property can be observed only by changing the composition of the substance"],1,"Burning gasoline is combustion: hydrocarbons react with O₂ to give CO₂ and H₂O - new substances, so it is a CHEMICAL change. The other four statements are true (frost is water vapor depositing as ice, a change of state).",["Find the statement that mislabels a process.","Combustion makes CO₂ and H₂O.","Is combustion just evaporation?"]],
["m1-a-067",1,1,1,"Concept","mcq","Which state of matter has a fixed volume but takes the shape of its container?",["solid","gas","both solid and gas","none of the three states","liquid"],4,"A liquid has a definite volume (particles stay close together) but flows to take the shape of its container. A solid keeps both its shape and volume; a gas fills its whole container, so it has neither a fixed shape nor a fixed volume.",["Pour water from a glass into a bowl: what changes and what does not?","The particles in this state are close but can move past each other.","Fixed volume, variable shape."]],
["m1-a-068",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","Why are gases much more compressible than liquids or solids?",["Gas particles are far apart, so most of a gas is empty space","Gas particles are smaller than the particles of liquids","Gas particles have no mass","Gas particles do not move","Gas particles are held in fixed positions"],0,"In a gas the particles are far apart and move randomly, so pressure can push them closer together. In liquids and solids the particles already touch, leaving little room to compress. Distractors: a substance's particles are the same size in every state; gas particles have mass and move rapidly.",["Picture the spacing of particles in each state.","Compression squeezes out empty space.","Which state has the most space between particles?"]],
["m1-a-069",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","Two oppositely charged particles are pulled farther apart. What happens to the potential energy of the pair?",["It decreases","It stays the same","It becomes zero","It increases","It is converted into mass"],3,"Opposite charges attract. Pulling them apart works against that attraction, so energy is stored as potential energy: the potential energy INCREASES. (Releasing them lets them move together, converting potential energy into kinetic energy.)",["Do opposite charges attract or repel?","Separating attracting particles takes work.","Work done against an attraction is stored as potential energy."]],
["m1-a-070",1,1,1,"Concept","mcq","How many significant figures are in 0.0055?",["4","5","2","3","1"],2,"Leading zeros only locate the decimal point and are never significant. In 0.0055 only the two 5s count: 2 significant figures (5.5 × 10⁻³). This is the lecture slide's example.",["Leading zeros are placeholders.","Write it in scientific notation.","5.5 × 10⁻³."]],
["m1-a-071",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","How many significant figures are in 0.06060 g?",["4","6","5","3","2"],0,"0.06060: the two leading zeros do not count; the 6, the captured 0 between the 6s, the second 6 and the trailing 0 (after a decimal point) all count: 4 significant figures (6.060 × 10⁻²).",["Ignore the leading zeros.","Zeros between nonzero digits count; trailing zeros count when there is a decimal point.","6.060 × 10⁻²."]],
["m1-a-072",1,1,2,"Trap","mcq","How many significant figures are in 5300 (written with no decimal point)?",["4","2","3","1","unlimited"],1,"Trailing zeros with NO decimal point are not significant, so 5300 has 2 significant figures (5.3 × 10³). Written \"5300.\" it would have 4. This exact pair is on the Lecture 1 slide.",["Is there a decimal point?","Trailing zeros without a decimal point are placeholders.","Compare 5300 with 5300."]],
["m1-a-073",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","How many significant figures are in 3040 kg?",["4","2","1","5","3"],4,"The 3, the captured 0 and the 4 are significant; the final 0 is a trailing zero with no decimal point, so it is not. 3040 kg has 3 significant figures (3.04 × 10³ kg).",["Captured zeros (between nonzero digits) always count.","The last zero has no decimal point after it.","3.04 × 10³."]],
["m1-a-074",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","How many significant figures are in 6.500 × 10⁻²?",["2","3","4","5","6"],2,"In scientific notation every digit of the coefficient is significant, and the exponent never counts. 6.500 has 4 significant figures. Writing 6.5 × 10⁻² would show only 2.",["Only the coefficient matters.","Trailing zeros after a decimal point count.","Count the digits in 6.500."]],
["m1-a-075",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","Which quantity is EXACT (has an unlimited number of significant figures)?",["1 kg = 2.205 lb","the speed of light, 2.998 × 10⁸ m/s","a mass of 12.00 g read from a balance","the 1000 mL in exactly 1 L","the density of water, 1.00 g/mL"],3,"Exact numbers come from definitions (1000 mL = 1 L, 2.54 cm = 1 in, 60 s = 1 min) or from counting. 1 kg = 2.205 lb is a ROUNDED conversion (4 significant figures). The speed of light, a balance reading and 1.00 g/mL are reported to a limited number of figures.",["Exact numbers are defined or counted, not measured.","Is 2.205 the full value of pounds per kilogram?","Metric prefixes define exact relationships."]],
["m1-a-076",1,1,3,"Concept","mcq","Which number has the MOST significant figures?",["0.000450","4.050 × 10³","40.050","405,000","0.04005"],2,"Counts: 0.000450 → 3 (4, 5, 0); 4.050 × 10³ → 4; 405,000 → 3 (trailing zeros, no decimal point); 40.050 → 5 (captured zeros and a trailing zero after the decimal point); 0.04005 → 4. The answer is 40.050.",["Count each one using the zero rules.","Leading zeros never count; trailing zeros count only with a decimal point.","One of them has five."]],
["m1-a-077",1,1,3,"Concept","mcq","Which number has exactly THREE significant figures?",["0.0300","300","3.000","0.003","30.03"],0,"0.0300: leading zeros do not count; the 3 and the two trailing zeros (after a decimal point) do: 3 significant figures (3.00 × 10⁻²). 300 has 1; 3.000 has 4; 0.003 has 1; 30.03 has 4.",["Apply the leading- and trailing-zero rules.","Trailing zeros count when there is a decimal point.","3.00 × 10⁻²."]],
["m1-a-078",1,1,3,"Trap","mcq","A measured time is written \"57,600. s\". Which is the same value in scientific notation with the correct number of significant figures?",["5.76 × 10⁴ s","5.760 × 10⁴ s","5.7600 × 10⁻⁴ s","57.600 × 10³ s","5.7600 × 10⁴ s"],4,"The decimal point after 57,600 makes both trailing zeros significant: 5 significant figures, so 5.7600 × 10⁴ s. Distractors: 5.76 and 5.760 drop significant zeros; 10⁻⁴ has the wrong sign; 57.600 × 10³ has the right digits but is not proper scientific notation (the coefficient must be between 1 and 10).",["The decimal point at the end matters.","How many significant figures does 57,600. have?","Keep all five digits in the coefficient."]],
["m1-a-079",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","Express 0.0000007160 cm³ in scientific notation with the correct number of significant figures.",["7.16 × 10⁻⁷ cm³","7.160 × 10⁻⁸ cm³","7.160 × 10⁷ cm³","7.160 × 10⁻⁷ cm³","7.160 × 10⁻⁶ cm³"],3,"Move the decimal point 7 places right to get 7.160, so the exponent is −7. The leading zeros vanish (not significant) but the trailing zero stays (significant): 4 significant figures, 7.160 × 10⁻⁷ cm³. Distractors: 7.16 drops a significant zero; −8, −6 and +7 miscount the decimal shift.",["Count how many places the decimal point moves.","A number less than 1 has a negative exponent.","The trailing zero in 0.0000007160 is significant."]],
["m1-a-080",1,1,1,"Lecture","mcq","When you record a reading from a ruler, buret or graduated cylinder, you should write down:",["only the digits known with certainty","all the digits known with certainty plus one estimated digit","all certain digits plus two estimated digits","as many digits as your calculator shows","the value of the nearest marked line only"],1,"Lecture 1: to record the uncertainty properly, a measurement uses all the known digits plus ONE estimated digit (the shell measured as 4.1 cm on a ruler marked in cm; the cylinder read as 17.5 mL). Those digits are the significant figures.",["The last digit of any measurement is estimated.","How many estimated digits does a measurement carry?","Certain digits + one guess between the lines."]],
["m1-a-081",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","A buret is marked every 0.1 mL. Which reading is recorded correctly?",["22.5 mL","22.473 mL","22.47 mL","22 mL","22.4700 mL"],2,"With marks every 0.1 mL, the tenths digit is certain and you estimate one more digit, the hundredths: 22.47 mL. 22.5 and 22 omit the estimated digit; 22.473 and 22.4700 claim precision the buret cannot give.",["The markings give the certain digits.","Estimate one digit beyond the smallest division.","Smallest division 0.1 mL → record to 0.01 mL."]],
["m1-a-082",1,1,3,"Lecture","mcq","Multiply and round to the correct number of significant figures: 3.951 × 0.83 = ?",["3.3","3.28","3.27933","3.27","3.279"],0,"3.951 × 0.83 = 3.27933. In multiplication the answer keeps the FEWEST significant figures of the data: 0.83 has 2, so the answer is 3.3. (This is the Lecture 1 clicker question.) Distractors keep too many digits, and 3.27 also truncates instead of rounding.",["Multiplication rule: fewest significant figures.","0.83 has two significant figures.","Round 3.27933 to two significant figures."]],
["m1-a-083",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","Round 9.786 to three significant figures.",["9.78","9.8","9.786","9.79","9.7"],3,"Keep 9.78, and look at the first digit removed: 6, which is more than 5, so the last kept digit goes up: 9.79. 9.78 truncates; 9.8 is two significant figures; 9.786 is four; 9.7 truncates to two.",["Keep three digits and look at the next one.","If the removed digit is more than 5, round up.","The digit after 9.78 is 6."]],
["m1-a-084",1,1,2,"Trap","mcq","Round 0.0063496 to three significant figures.",["0.00634","0.006","0.0063","0.006350","0.00635"],4,"The first three significant figures are 6, 3, 4; the next digit is 9, so 4 rounds up to 5: 0.00635. Distractors: 0.00634 truncates; 0.006 and 0.0063 keep too few; 0.006350 adds a fourth significant figure. Leading zeros are not counted.",["Leading zeros do not count - start with the 6.","Keep 6, 3, 4 and look at the next digit.","The next digit is 9."]],
["m1-a-085",1,1,3,"Trap","mcq","Round 1.4564 to two significant figures.",["1.4","1.5","1.46","1.45","1.456"],1,"Keep 1.4; the removed digits are 564. The first removed digit is 5 and it is followed by NONZERO digits, so the value is above the halfway point and rounds up: 1.5 (the lecture's own example). 1.4 treats it as an exact half or truncates; the others keep too many digits.",["Look at all the digits being removed, not just the first.","0.0564 is more than half of 0.1.","A 5 followed by nonzero digits always rounds up."]],
["m1-a-086",1,1,3,"Lecture","mcq","The CHM 11510 Lecture 1 rounding rules say: if the digit removed is 5 followed only by zeros (or nothing), the preceding digit goes up if it is odd and stays the same if it is even. Using that rule, round 1.4500 to two significant figures.",["1.5","1.45","1.4","1.450","1.40"],2,"The digits removed are 500 - an exact 5 followed by zeros - and the preceding digit 4 is EVEN, so it stays: 1.4 (the slide's example; 1.75 → 1.8 because 7 is odd). Note: the 10th-edition textbook text instead says a removed 5 always rounds up, which would give 1.5; the stem here asks for the lecture rule. Exam choices rarely hinge on this case.",["Is the removed part exactly half (5 then zeros)?","Then look at whether the digit before it is odd or even.","4 is even."]],
["m1-a-087",1,1,3,"Lecture","mcq","Your calculator shows 2458.72 for a speed that should have three significant figures. How should you report it?",["2.45 × 10³","2.46 × 10³","246","2459","2.4587 × 10³"],1,"Three significant figures: 2, 4, 5, and the next digit (8) rounds the 5 up → 2.46 × 10³. Writing \"2460\" would be ambiguous about the trailing zero, so scientific notation is clearest. Distractors: 2.45 × 10³ truncates; 246 loses the magnitude (it is 2460, not 246); 2459 and 2.4587 × 10³ keep too many digits.",["Keep three digits and check the fourth.","Use scientific notation to avoid an ambiguous trailing zero.","2458.72 → 2.46 × 10³."]],
["m1-a-088",1,1,2,"Calculation","mcq","A sheet of material measures 9.2 cm × 6.8 cm × 0.3744 cm. What is its volume, correctly rounded?",["23 cm³","23.4 cm³","23.42 cm³","23.422464 cm³","2 × 10¹ cm³"],0,"9.2 × 6.8 × 0.3744 = 23.422464 cm³. Multiplication keeps the fewest significant figures: 9.2 and 6.8 each have 2, so V = 23 cm³. The precise thickness cannot rescue the two-figure length and width.",["Multiplication/division rule.","Find the measurement with the fewest significant figures.","Two significant figures."]],
["m1-a-089",1,1,2,"Calculation","mcq","You add 23.28 mL of water from a buret to 83.5 mL of solution in a graduated cylinder. What total volume should you report?",["106.78 mL","107 mL","106.780 mL","106.8 mL","1.1 × 10² mL"],3,"83.5 + 23.28 = 106.78 mL. Addition keeps the fewest DECIMAL PLACES: 83.5 has one, so the answer is 106.8 mL (four significant figures - more than 83.5 has, which is fine for addition). Distractors: 107 mL wrongly applies the \"three significant figures\" rule from multiplication.",["Addition rule: decimal places, not significant figures.","83.5 has one decimal place.","Round 106.78 to the tenths place."]],
["m1-a-090",1,1,2,"Lecture","mcq","Add and round correctly: 197.843 + 98.7 = ?",["296.543","296.54","297","3.0 × 10²","296.5"],4,"197.843 + 98.7 = 296.543. The answer keeps the fewest decimal places of the data: 98.7 has one, so 296.5 (the Lecture 1 example). 297 wrongly uses the significant-figure count of 98.7.",["Addition rule.","Which number has the fewest decimal places?","One decimal place."]],
["m1-a-091",1,1,2,"Lecture","mcq","Multiply and round correctly: 1.9 × 87.6 = ?",["166.44","166.4","166","1.66 × 10²","1.7 × 10²"],4,"1.9 × 87.6 = 166.44. Multiplication keeps the fewest significant figures: 1.9 has 2, so the answer is 1.7 × 10² (the slide writes it as 170). The others keep three or more figures.",["Multiplication rule.","1.9 has two significant figures.","Round 166.44 to two significant figures."]],
["m1-a-092",1,1,4,"Trap","mcq","Evaluate (35.62 − 32.1) ÷ 2.115 and report the answer with the correct number of significant figures.",["1.66","1.664","1.6643","1.7","2"],3,"Do the subtraction first: 35.62 − 32.1 = 3.52, but 32.1 has one decimal place, so the difference is good to the tenths place: 3.5 (TWO significant figures). Then 3.52 ÷ 2.115 = 1.6643, which must be reported to two significant figures: 1.7. Trap: 2.115 has four figures, but the subtraction step already limited the result.",["Mixed operations: do the subtraction first and note its precision.","3.52 is only good to one decimal place - how many significant figures is that?","Two significant figures in the final answer."]],
["m1-a-093",1,1,4,"Calculation","mcq","Evaluate (8.21 + 0.0155) × 4.1 and report the answer with the correct number of significant figures.",["33.7","33.72","34","33.725","30"],2,"Addition first: 8.21 + 0.0155 = 8.2255 → good to two decimal places (8.23, three significant figures). Then × 4.1: 8.2255 × 4.1 = 33.725. 4.1 has two significant figures, so the answer is 34. Distractors keep too many figures; \"30\" has only one.",["Addition step: decimal places. Multiplication step: significant figures.","The sum has three significant figures; 4.1 has two.","Two significant figures in the end."]],
["m1-a-094",1,1,3,"Calculation","mcq","Three weighings of the same object give 10.25 g, 10.31 g and 10.28 g. What is the average mass, correctly reported?",["10.28 g","10.3 g","10.280 g","10 g","10.2800 g"],0,"Sum = 10.25 + 10.31 + 10.28 = 30.84 g (two decimal places). Dividing by 3 - an exact count - does not limit the figures: 30.84 / 3 = 10.28 g. Distractors: 10.3 drops a significant figure; 10 treats the count 3 as a one-significant-figure measurement; 10.280 and 10.2800 add figures the data do not have.",["Add first (decimal-place rule), then divide by the count.","The 3 is a counted, exact number.","Keep four significant figures."]],
["m1-a-095",1,1,3,"Lecture","mcq","You are driving at 55.0 mi/h. What is your speed in cm/s? (1 mi = 5280 ft, 1 ft = 12 in, 1 in = 2.54 cm, all exact)",["2458.72 cm/s","2.46 × 10³ cm/s","2.5 × 10³ cm/s","1.48 × 10⁵ cm/s","8.85 × 10⁶ cm/s"],1,"55.0 mi/h × 5280 ft/mi × 12 in/ft × 2.54 cm/in × 1 h/3600 s = 2458.72 cm/s → 2.46 × 10³ cm/s (the Lecture 1 example: 55.0 has three significant figures; the conversion factors are exact). Distractors: 2458.72 cm/s is unrounded; 2.5 × 10³ has only two figures; 1.48 × 10⁵ divides by 60 only (minutes); 8.85 × 10⁶ never converts hours to seconds.",["Chain the length conversions mi → ft → in → cm.","Then convert hours to seconds: 1 h = 3600 s.","Three significant figures from 55.0."]],
["m1-a-096",1,1,4,"Trap","mcq","A student adds 2.35 g to a mass measured as 1.0 × 10³ g. How should the total mass be reported?",["1002.35 g","1.0 × 10³ g","1002.4 g","1002 g","1.00 × 10³ g"],1,"1.0 × 10³ g is known only to the HUNDREDS place (two significant figures: 1.0 thousand). Addition keeps the precision of the least precise term, so 1000 + 2.35 = 1002.35 must be rounded to the hundreds place: 1.0 × 10³ g. The 2.35 g is lost in the uncertainty. Distractors all claim precision in the tens place or beyond, which the large mass does not have.",["Where is the last significant digit of 1.0 × 10³?","Write 1.0 × 10³ as a number with its uncertain digit marked: 1,0̲00.","Round 1002.35 to the hundreds place."]],
["m1-a-097",1,1,3,"Calculation","mcq","A calorimetry calculation gives q = 4.184 J/(g·°C) × 50.0 g × 6.7 °C. How should q be reported?",["1401.6 J","1.40 × 10³ J","1.4 × 10³ J","1402 J","1 × 10³ J"],2,"4.184 × 50.0 × 6.7 = 1401.6 J. The temperature change 6.7 °C has only two significant figures, so q = 1.4 × 10³ J. This is why a ΔT measured to only two figures limits a whole thermochemistry answer.",["Multiplication rule.","Find the factor with the fewest significant figures.","6.7 has two significant figures."]],
["m1-a-098",1,1,1,"Concept","mcq","In measurement, PRECISION refers to:",["how closely repeated measurements agree with one another","how close a measurement is to the true value","the number of digits a calculator displays","the absence of systematic error","the difference between the largest measurement and the true value"],0,"Precision (reproducibility) is the closeness of measurements in a series to EACH OTHER. Accuracy is closeness to the ACTUAL value. A set can be precise without being accurate (tightly clustered around the wrong value).",["Two words, two meanings: accuracy and precision.","One compares measurements with each other, the other with the true value.","Reproducibility is the other name for this one."]],
["m1-a-099",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","The true mass of an object is 25.00 g. Four students each weigh it three times: Set A: 25.01, 24.99, 25.00 g; Set B: 26.02, 26.01, 26.03 g; Set C: 24.50, 25.60, 24.90 g; Set D: 26.90, 25.70, 27.20 g. Which set is precise but NOT accurate?",["Set A","Set C","Set D","Set B","Sets A and B"],3,"Set B values agree within 0.02 g (precise) but all sit about 1.02 g above 25.00 g (not accurate - a systematic error). Set A is precise AND accurate (so \"Sets A and B\" is wrong). Set C scatters widely but averages 25.00 g; Set D scatters and averages 26.60 g.",["Precise = close to each other. Accurate = close to 25.00 g.","Look for a tight cluster in the wrong place.","One set is consistently about 1 g high."]],
["m1-a-100",1,1,3,"Concept","mcq","The true mass of an object is 25.00 g. Four students each weigh it three times: Set A: 25.01, 24.99, 25.00 g; Set B: 26.02, 26.01, 26.03 g; Set C: 24.50, 25.60, 24.90 g; Set D: 26.90, 25.70, 27.20 g. Which set shows large random error but an AVERAGE close to the true value?",["Set A","Set B","Set D","Sets C and D","Set C"],4,"Averages: A 25.00, B 26.02, C (24.50 + 25.60 + 24.90)/3 = 25.00, D (26.90 + 25.70 + 27.20)/3 = 26.60 g. C and D both scatter by about 1 g (large random error, low precision), but only C's average lands on the true value. D also has a systematic error.",["Large random error = widely scattered values.","Compute the averages of the two scattered sets.","Only one scattered set averages 25.00 g."]],
["m1-a-101",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","A balance reads 0.50 g too high on every weighing. What kind of error is this, and what does it affect?",["random error; it lowers precision","systematic error; it lowers precision only","random error; it lowers accuracy only","systematic error; it lowers accuracy","no error, because the readings are reproducible"],3,"An error that pushes every reading the same way (all high or all low) is systematic, typically from faulty or uncalibrated equipment. It makes results inaccurate even if they are perfectly reproducible (precise). Random error scatters values above and below and lowers precision.",["Are the readings off in the same direction every time?","Same direction every time = systematic.","Systematic error hurts accuracy."]],
["m1-a-102",1,1,3,"Concept","mcq","Which action best reduces a SYSTEMATIC error in a measurement?",["averaging more repeated trials","recording more digits from the display","calibrating the instrument against a known standard","carrying extra digits through the calculation","rounding only at the end of the calculation"],2,"Systematic error shifts every reading the same way, so averaging cannot remove it; it is corrected by calibration - comparing the device with a known standard. Averaging more trials reduces the effect of RANDOM error; the other choices are about sig-fig bookkeeping, not measurement error.",["Does averaging help if every reading is too high by the same amount?","You need to find out how far off the instrument is.","Compare the device with a standard of known value."]],
["m1-a-103",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","Large RANDOM error in a set of measurements results in:",["low precision","low accuracy only, with high precision","values that are all too high","values that are all too low","no effect, because random errors cancel in each measurement"],0,"Random error scatters values above and below the actual value, so the measurements disagree with each other: low precision. (Their average can still be close to the true value.) All-high or all-low values are the signature of systematic error.",["Random = sometimes high, sometimes low.","Scattered values disagree with each other.","Disagreement among repeats is low ___."]],
["m1-a-104",1,1,4,"Exam","mcq","The true density of aluminum is 2.70 g/cm³. Four students measure it three times each. Student 1: 2.71, 2.69, 2.70. Student 2: 2.95, 2.94, 2.96. Student 3: 2.55, 2.88, 2.67. Student 4: 2.31, 2.62, 2.40 (all g/cm³). Which statement is FALSE?",["Student 2's results show a systematic error","Student 2's results are more accurate than Student 3's because they are more precise","Student 3's average is accurate even though the results are imprecise","Student 1's results are both precise and accurate","Student 4's results have low precision and low accuracy"],1,"Averages: Student 1 = 2.70, Student 2 = 2.95, Student 3 = 2.70, Student 4 = 2.44 g/cm³. Student 2 is precise (spread 0.02) but every value is about 0.25 high - a systematic error, so NOT accurate. Student 3 scatters (imprecise) but averages 2.70, the true value, so Student 3 is the more accurate. Precision does not imply accuracy - that statement is false. The other four statements are true.",["Compute each student's average and spread.","Accuracy compares the average with 2.70; precision compares the values with each other.","Does being tightly clustered make a result correct?"]],
["m1-a-105",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","A thermometer always reads 2 °C higher than the true temperature. Which description fits measurements made with it?",["They are accurate but not precise","They are neither precise nor accurate, because the error is random","They are accurate, because the error is the same every time","They have only random error","They can be precise but are not accurate"],4,"A constant offset is a systematic error: repeated readings can agree closely (precise) while all being 2 °C too high (inaccurate). Calibration would fix it.",["Is the error the same every time or scattered?","A constant offset is systematic.","Systematic error affects accuracy, not reproducibility."]],
["m1-a-106",1,1,3,"Calculation","mcq","Speaker wire costs $1.25 per foot. What is the cost of 325 cm of wire? (2.54 cm = 1 in, 12 in = 1 ft)",["$406","$33.9","$160","$13.3","$86.0"],3,"325 cm × (1 in / 2.54 cm) × (1 ft / 12 in) × ($1.25 / 1 ft) = 10.66 ft × $1.25/ft = $13.33 → $13.3. Distractors: $406 never converts the length; $33.9 skips cm → in; $160 skips in → ft; $86.0 multiplies by 2.54 instead of dividing.",["Get the length in feet, because the price is per foot.","cm → in → ft, arranging each factor so units cancel.","Then multiply by $1.25 per foot."]],
["m1-a-107",1,1,3,"Calculation","mcq","A drug is prescribed at 2.5 mg per kg of body mass. How many milligrams should a 165 lb patient receive? (1 kg = 2.205 lb)",["4.1 × 10² mg","1.9 × 10² mg","9.1 × 10² mg","66 mg","1.87 × 10² mg"],1,"165 lb × (1 kg / 2.205 lb) = 74.83 kg; × 2.5 mg/kg = 187.1 mg → 1.9 × 10² mg (2.5 mg/kg has two significant figures). Distractors: 4.1 × 10² mg uses pounds as if they were kg; 9.1 × 10² mg multiplies by 2.205; 66 mg divides by the dose; 1.87 × 10² mg keeps three figures.",["The dose is per kilogram, so convert the patient's mass to kg.","Divide pounds by 2.205.","Then multiply by 2.5 mg/kg and round to two significant figures."]],
["m1-a-108",1,1,4,"Exam","mcq","A child of mass 22.0 kg needs an antibiotic dose of 15 mg per kg. The liquid suspension contains 125 mg of drug per 5.00 mL. What volume of suspension should be given?",["66 mL","2.6 mL","8.2 × 10³ mL","0.60 mL","13 mL"],4,"Dose = 22.0 kg × 15 mg/kg = 330 mg. Volume = 330 mg × (5.00 mL / 125 mg) = 13.2 mL → 13 mL (15 mg/kg has two significant figures). Distractors: 66 mL divides by 5.00 instead of using the concentration; 2.6 mL divides by 125 but forgets the 5.00 mL; 8.2 × 10³ mL inverts the concentration (8250, with the exact 5 rounded to even as in Lecture 1); 0.60 mL ignores the child's mass.",["First find the dose in mg.","Use the label as a conversion factor: 125 mg = 5.00 mL.","330 mg × 5.00 mL/125 mg."]],
["m1-a-109",1,1,3,"Calculation","mcq","An IV line delivers 2.0 drops per second, and each drop has a mass of 55 mg. What mass, in kilograms, is delivered in 6.0 hours?",["2.4 kg","2.4 × 10³ kg","0.040 kg","6.6 × 10⁻⁴ kg","2.4 × 10⁶ kg"],0,"6.0 h × 3600 s/h = 21,600 s. Drops = 2.0 drops/s × 21,600 s = 43,200 drops. Mass = 43,200 × 55 mg = 2.376 × 10⁶ mg × (1 g / 1000 mg) × (1 kg / 1000 g) = 2.376 → 2.4 kg. Distractors: 2.4 × 10³ kg converts mg → g but calls it kg; 0.040 kg converts hours to minutes only; 6.6 × 10⁻⁴ kg never converts hours to seconds; 2.4 × 10⁶ kg skips mg → kg.",["The rate is per second, so convert the time to seconds.","drops/s × s → drops; × mg/drop → mg.","mg → kg is ÷ 10⁶."]],
["m1-a-110",1,1,3,"Calculation","num","Convert a speed of 55.0 mi/h to meters per second. (1 mi = 5280 ft, 1 ft = 12 in, 1 in = 2.54 cm, all exact; enter a number in m/s.)",["24.6 m/s"],24.6,"55.0 mi/h × 5280 ft/mi × 12 in/ft × 2.54 cm/in × (1 m/100 cm) × (1 h/3600 s) = 24.587 → 24.6 m/s. Trap: forgetting the 3600 s/h gives 8.85 × 10⁴ m/s.",["Convert the distance unit and the time unit separately.","mi → ft → in → cm → m on top; h → s on the bottom.","1 mi = 1609.344 m exactly; divide by 3600."]],
["m1-a-111",1,1,3,"Calculation","mcq","A professor walks one mile in 15 minutes. At this pace, how many minutes does it take to walk 10,500 m? (1 mi = 1.609 km)",["2.5 × 10² min","6.5 min","98 min","1.6 × 10⁵ min","97.89 min"],2,"10,500 m = 10.5 km. 10.5 km × (1 mi / 1.609 km) = 6.526 mi. × 15 min/mi = 97.89 min → 98 min (15 min has two significant figures). Distractors: 2.5 × 10² min multiplies by 1.609; 6.5 min stops at miles; 1.6 × 10⁵ min treats meters as miles; 97.89 min keeps too many figures.",["Convert the distance into miles.","m → km → mi.","Then multiply by 15 min per mile; two significant figures."]],
["m1-a-112",1,1,3,"Calculation","mcq","A standard apple contains 159 mg of potassium, and there are 3 apples per pound (treat as exact). How many grams of potassium are in 3.25 kg of apples? (1 kg = 2.205 lb)",["0.703 g","3.42 × 10³ g","3.42 g","1.14 g","0.380 g"],2,"3.25 kg × 2.205 lb/kg = 7.166 lb; × 3 apples/lb = 21.50 apples; × 159 mg/apple = 3418.3 mg = 3.42 g. Distractors: 0.703 g divides by 2.205; 3.42 × 10³ g leaves the answer in mg; 1.14 g forgets the 3 apples per pound; 0.380 g divides by 3.",["Plan the road map: kg → lb → apples → mg K → g K.","There are 2.205 lb in a kg (so the number of pounds is larger).","Remember the final mg → g step."]],
["m1-a-113",1,1,4,"Exam","mcq","A car averages 28.5 mi per gallon and gasoline costs $3.89 per gallon. What is the fuel cost of a 452 km trip? (1 mi = 1.609 km)",["$38.3","$61.7","$99.3","$3.11 × 10⁴","$9.86"],0,"452 km × (1 mi / 1.609 km) = 280.9 mi; ÷ 28.5 mi/gal = 9.857 gal; × $3.89/gal = $38.34 → $38.3. Distractors: $61.7 treats km as miles; $99.3 multiplies by 1.609; $3.11 × 10⁴ multiplies by the mileage instead of dividing; $9.86 is the number of gallons, not the cost.",["km → mi → gal → $.","Miles per gallon is used upside down: gal/mi.","Check that the units cancel to dollars."]],
["m1-a-114",1,1,4,"Trap","mcq","One gallon of paint covers 3.50 × 10² ft². How many gallons are needed to paint 125 m² of wall? (1 ft = 0.3048 m exactly)",["1.17 gal","0.357 gal","0.0332 gal","1.35 × 10³ gal","3.84 gal"],4,"1 ft² = (0.3048 m)² = 0.09290 m². 125 m² × (1 ft² / 0.09290 m²) = 1345.5 ft². ÷ 350 ft²/gal = 3.844 → 3.84 gal. Distractors: 1.17 gal converts with 0.3048 unsquared; 0.357 gal never converts m² to ft²; 0.0332 gal multiplies by 0.0929; 1.35 × 10³ is the area in ft², not gallons.",["The area must be in ft² to use the coverage.","Square the length factor: (0.3048 m)² = 1 ft².","ft² ÷ 350 ft²/gal."]],
["m1-a-115",1,1,4,"Exam","mcq","A furniture maker receives 4 bolts of fabric, each holding exactly 150 m². Each chair needs 27.0 ft² of fabric. How many COMPLETE chairs can be upholstered? (1 ft = 0.3048 m exactly)",["72 chairs","22 chairs","2 chairs","239 chairs","6458 chairs"],3,"Fabric = 4 × 150 m² = 600 m². 1 ft² = (0.3048)² m² = 0.09290 m², so 600 m² = 6458.3 ft². Chairs = 6458.3 ÷ 27.0 = 239.2 → 239 complete chairs. Distractors: 72 squares only the unit, not 0.3048 (the textbook's highlighted error); 22 skips the conversion; 2 multiplies by the factor; 6458 forgets to divide by 27.0 ft² per chair.",["Total fabric in m² first.","Convert m² to ft² with (0.3048)², not 0.3048.","Divide by 27.0 ft² per chair and keep whole chairs."]],
["m1-a-116",1,1,3,"Calculation","num","A virus particle has a diameter of 25 nm. How many of these particles could line up side by side along a line 1.0 cm long? (Enter a number.)",["4.0 × 10⁵ particles"],400000,"1.0 cm = 1.0 × 10⁻² m and 25 nm = 25 × 10⁻⁹ m = 2.5 × 10⁻⁸ m. Number = (1.0 × 10⁻² m) ÷ (2.5 × 10⁻⁸ m per particle) = 4.0 × 10⁵ particles. Trap: dividing 1.0 by 25 without converting units gives 0.040.",["Put both lengths in the same unit.","cm → m (× 10⁻²); nm → m (× 10⁻⁹).","Divide the line length by one particle's diameter."]],
["m1-a-117",1,1,2,"Concept","mcq","Write 0.000470 in scientific notation with the correct number of significant figures.",["4.7 × 10⁻⁴","4.70 × 10⁻⁴","4.70 × 10⁴","47.0 × 10⁻⁵","4.70 × 10⁻³"],1,"Move the decimal point 4 places right: 4.70 × 10⁻⁴. The trailing zero is significant (3 significant figures) and must stay. Distractors: 4.7 drops it; 10⁴ has the wrong sign; 47.0 × 10⁻⁵ is not proper notation; 10⁻³ miscounts the places.",["Count the places the decimal point moves.","Small numbers get negative exponents.","Keep the trailing zero - it is significant."]],
["m1-a-118",1,1,2,"Calculation","mcq","Calculate (3.0 × 10⁴)(2.0 × 10⁻⁶).",["6.0 × 10⁻²⁴","6.0 × 10¹⁰","6.0 × 10⁻²","5.0 × 10⁻²","6.0 × 10²"],2,"Multiply the coefficients (3.0 × 2.0 = 6.0) and ADD the exponents (4 + (−6) = −2): 6.0 × 10⁻². Distractors: −24 multiplies the exponents; 10¹⁰ subtracts them; 5.0 adds the coefficients; 10² drops the minus sign.",["Coefficients multiply; exponents add.","4 + (−6) = ?","3.0 × 2.0 = 6.0."]],
["m1-a-119",1,1,2,"Calculation","mcq","Calculate (6.022 × 10²³) ÷ (2.0 × 10³) with the correct number of significant figures.",["3.011 × 10²⁰","3.0 × 10²⁰","3.0 × 10²⁶","1.2 × 10²⁷","3.0 × 10¹⁹"],1,"Divide the coefficients (6.022 ÷ 2.0 = 3.011) and SUBTRACT the exponents (23 − 3 = 20): 3.011 × 10²⁰ → 3.0 × 10²⁰ (2.0 has two significant figures). Distractors: 3.011 keeps too many figures; 10²⁶ adds the exponents; 1.2 × 10²⁷ multiplies; 10¹⁹ miscounts.",["Division: divide coefficients, subtract exponents.","23 − 3 = 20.","Two significant figures from 2.0."]],
["m2-a-001",2,2,1,"Concept","mcq","Which of the following is a COMPOUND?",["oxygen gas","brass","air","seawater","sodium chloride"],4,"A compound is a substance of two or more elements chemically combined in a fixed ratio: sodium chloride is always NaCl. Oxygen gas (O₂) is an element; brass (copper + zinc), air and seawater are mixtures whose composition can vary.",["Is it a pure substance or a mixture?","A compound has a fixed composition of two or more elements.","Which choice has a single chemical formula with two elements?"]],
["m2-a-002",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which of the following is a HOMOGENEOUS mixture?",["pure water","oil and water","granite","salt dissolved in water","sodium chloride"],3,"A homogeneous mixture (a solution) has the same composition throughout and no visible boundaries: salt water. Pure water and sodium chloride are pure substances (compounds), not mixtures. Oil and water and granite are heterogeneous - you can see the different parts.",["First rule out the pure substances.","Homogeneous = uniform throughout (a solution).","Which mixture looks like a single phase?"]],
["m2-a-003",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which of the following is a HETEROGENEOUS mixture?",["oil-and-vinegar salad dressing","brass","air","sugar dissolved in water","pure ethanol"],0,"Oil and vinegar separate into visible layers, so the mixture is not uniform: heterogeneous. Brass (a solid solution), air and sugar water are homogeneous mixtures; pure ethanol is a compound.",["Heterogeneous = you can see different regions.","Alloys and gas mixtures are uniform.","Which mixture separates into layers?"]],
["m2-a-004",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which statement correctly distinguishes a compound from a mixture?",["A compound can be separated into its elements by filtration; a mixture cannot","A compound has a fixed composition by mass; a mixture's composition can vary","A mixture always contains only one element","A compound keeps the properties of its component elements","A mixture always contains exactly two substances"],1,"By the law of definite composition, every sample of a compound has the same mass fractions of its elements. A mixture's components can be present in any proportion and keep their own properties. Compounds have properties DIFFERENT from their elements (NaCl is nothing like Na metal or Cl₂ gas) and can only be broken down chemically.",["Think of salt water made with more or less salt.","Compounds obey the law of definite composition.","Fixed vs variable composition."]],
["m2-a-005",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which technique would recover pure water from salt water?",["filtration","chromatography","distillation","decanting","using a magnet"],2,"Distillation separates components by differences in volatility (boiling point): the water boils off and is condensed, leaving the salt behind. Filtration only removes undissolved solids - dissolved salt passes through the filter. Chromatography separates by differing attraction to a stationary phase and is used for small analytical samples.",["Is the salt dissolved or suspended?","Water and salt have very different boiling points.","Boil, then condense the vapor."]],
["m2-a-006",2,2,1,"Concept","mcq","Which technique separates sand from water?",["filtration","distillation of the sand","chromatography","crystallization","electrolysis"],0,"Sand does not dissolve, so passing the mixture through a filter paper holds back the solid and lets the water through. Electrolysis is a chemical change, not a separation of a mixture.",["Is the sand dissolved?","An insoluble solid can be caught by a porous barrier.","Use filter paper."]],
["m2-a-007",2,2,1,"Concept","mcq","A substance that cannot be broken down into simpler substances by chemical means is:",["a compound","a homogeneous mixture","a heterogeneous mixture","an element","a molecule of a compound"],3,"An element consists of only one kind of atom and cannot be decomposed chemically. Compounds can be decomposed into their elements (water → H₂ + O₂ by electrolysis); mixtures can be separated physically.",["Think of the simplest kind of pure substance.","Compounds CAN be broken down chemically.","Only one kind of atom."]],
["m2-a-008",2,2,3,"Concept","mcq","A pure liquid is decomposed by an electric current into two different gases, which recombine to give back the original liquid. The original liquid must be:",["an element","a homogeneous mixture","a heterogeneous mixture","an alloy","a compound"],4,"Being broken down by a CHEMICAL process (electrolysis) into simpler substances means it is not an element; being a single pure substance with a fixed composition (it re-forms exactly) makes it a compound - water is the classic example. A mixture would be separated by physical means.",["Elements cannot be decomposed chemically.","The liquid is described as pure.","Pure + chemically decomposable = ?"]],
["m2-a-009",2,2,1,"Concept","mcq","Which statement is the law of DEFINITE COMPOSITION?",["The total mass of substances does not change during a chemical reaction","A compound always contains the same elements in the same proportions by mass","When two elements form more than one compound, the masses of one that combine with a fixed mass of the other are in small whole-number ratios","Atoms of an element are identical in mass","Equal volumes of gases contain equal numbers of particles"],1,"Definite (constant) composition: no matter its source, a compound has the same elements in the same parts by mass (NO₂ is always 30.45% N). The first distractor is mass conservation, the second is multiple proportions, the fourth is one of Dalton's postulates, and the last is Avogadro's hypothesis.",["Three mass laws: conservation, definite composition, multiple proportions.","This one is about a SINGLE compound.","Fixed mass fractions."]],
["m2-a-010",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Every sample of pure water, from any source, contains 11.19% hydrogen by mass. This illustrates:",["the law of mass conservation","the law of multiple proportions","the law of definite composition","the existence of isotopes","the nuclear model of the atom"],2,"A fixed mass percentage of each element in a compound regardless of source is the law of definite composition. Multiple proportions needs TWO compounds of the same elements; mass conservation is about mass before and after a reaction.",["How many compounds are involved?","The statement is about one compound always having the same make-up.","Same proportions → definite composition."]],
["m2-a-011",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","When 10.0 g of calcium carbonate is heated, it forms 5.60 g of calcium oxide and 4.40 g of carbon dioxide. Which law do these data illustrate?",["the law of mass conservation","the law of definite composition","the law of multiple proportions","Dalton's postulate that atoms are indivisible","the law of constant volume"],0,"5.60 g + 4.40 g = 10.00 g: the total mass of the products equals the mass of the reactant, which is the law of mass conservation. Nothing here compares the composition of different samples (definite composition) or of two compounds of the same elements (multiple proportions).",["Add up the product masses.","Compare with the reactant mass.","Mass before = mass after."]],
["m2-a-012",2,2,2,"Calculation","mcq","In a sealed flask, 2.43 g of magnesium reacts completely with oxygen to form 4.03 g of magnesium oxide. What mass of oxygen reacted?",["6.46 g","1.60 g","0.603 g","1.66 g","2.43 g"],1,"Mass is conserved: mass of Mg + mass of O₂ = mass of MgO, so mass of O₂ = 4.03 g − 2.43 g = 1.60 g. Distractors: 6.46 g adds the masses; 0.603 and 1.66 are ratios, not masses; 2.43 g assumes equal masses react.",["Mass conservation: reactant masses add to the product mass.","Mg + O₂ → MgO.","Subtract 2.43 g from 4.03 g."]],
["m2-a-013",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Wood burned in an open fire leaves ashes that weigh much less than the wood. Does this violate the law of mass conservation?",["Yes - mass is destroyed when wood burns","Yes - the law applies only to physical changes","No - because the ashes have a higher density than the wood","No - because mass conservation applies only to elements, not compounds","No - gases such as CO₂ and H₂O escaped; in a closed container the total mass would not change"],4,"The products of combustion include gases that leave the open fire. If all reactants (including O₂) and all products were weighed in a sealed container, the total mass would be unchanged. Mass conservation applies to every chemical reaction.",["Where did the \"missing\" mass go?","Combustion produces gases.","Weigh everything, including the gases, in a closed system."]],
["m2-a-014",2,2,4,"Calculation","mcq","A 95.0 g sample of \"fool's gold\" (a compound of only iron and sulfur) contains 44.2 g of iron. What mass of sulfur is in a sample of fool's gold that contains 60.0 g of iron?",["129 g","52.2 g","69.0 g","32.1 g","35.0 g"],2,"By definite composition the S:Fe mass ratio is the same in every sample. First sample: S = 95.0 − 44.2 = 50.8 g, so S/Fe = 50.8/44.2 = 1.149. Second sample: S = 60.0 g Fe × 1.149 = 69.0 g. Distractors: 129 g is the mass of the whole second sample; 52.2 g inverts the ratio; 32.1 g treats 60.0 g as the mass of the whole sample; 35.0 g subtracts unrelated masses.",["Find the mass of sulfur in the first sample by difference.","The ratio (g S)/(g Fe) is fixed for this compound.","Multiply that ratio by the new mass of iron."]],
["m2-a-015",2,2,3,"Calculation","num","A 31.4 g sample of silver bromide contains 18.0 g of silver, with bromine the only other element. What mass of bromine is in 5.25 g of silver bromide? (Enter a number in g.)",["2.24 g"],2.24,"Mass of Br in the analysed sample = 31.4 − 18.0 = 13.4 g, so the mass fraction of Br = 13.4/31.4 = 0.4268. In 5.25 g: 5.25 g × 0.4268 = 2.240 → 2.24 g Br. (The silver is 3.01 g; the two add to 5.25 g.) Trap: using the silver fraction gives 3.01 g.",["Definite composition: the mass fraction of Br is fixed.","Mass fraction = mass of element ÷ mass of compound.","Multiply 5.25 g by (13.4 g / 31.4 g)."]],
["m2-a-016",2,2,4,"Trap","mcq","A 50.0 g sample of a uranium oxide contains 42.4 g of uranium. What mass of oxygen is in 250. kg of this oxide?",["212 kg","295 kg","45 kg","38 kg","0.152 kg"],3,"Oxygen in the analysed sample = 50.0 − 42.4 = 7.6 g. Subtraction keeps one decimal place, so 7.6 g has only TWO significant figures. Mass fraction O = 7.6/50.0 = 0.152; 250. kg × 0.152 = 38 kg (two significant figures). Distractors: 212 kg is the uranium; 295 kg inverts the uranium fraction; 45 kg uses the O:U ratio instead of the O:oxide fraction; 0.152 kg is the mass fraction itself.",["Find the mass of oxygen in the 50.0 g sample.","Mass fraction of O = (g O)/(g oxide), and the same fraction applies in kg.","Watch the significant figures of 50.0 − 42.4."]],
["m2-a-017",2,2,3,"Calculation","mcq","Two samples of a white solid are analysed. Sample 1: 1.50 g of solid contains 0.600 g of calcium. Sample 2: 4.00 g of solid contains 1.60 g of calcium. What can you conclude?",["Both are 40.0% calcium, consistent with the same compound (definite composition)","They must be different compounds because the masses of calcium differ","Sample 2 is 160% calcium, so it is impure","They illustrate the law of multiple proportions","They show that mass is not conserved"],0,"Mass fraction of Ca: sample 1 = 0.600/1.50 = 0.400; sample 2 = 1.60/4.00 = 0.400. Equal mass fractions are exactly what definite composition predicts for samples of one compound. Different SAMPLE sizes naturally contain different masses of Ca. Multiple proportions would need two different compounds of the same elements.",["Compare mass fractions, not raw masses.","Fraction = mass of Ca ÷ mass of sample.","Both come to 0.400."]],
["m2-a-018",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which pair of compounds illustrates the law of MULTIPLE PROPORTIONS?",["NaCl and NaBr","SO₂ and SO₃","O₂ and O₃","H₂O and H₂S","CH₄ and SiH₄"],1,"Multiple proportions needs the SAME two elements forming more than one compound. In SO₂ and SO₃, the masses of O combining with a fixed mass of S are in the ratio 2 : 3. NaCl/NaBr, H₂O/H₂S and CH₄/SiH₄ each involve different element pairs; O₂ and O₃ are two forms of one element, not compounds.",["Both compounds must contain exactly the same two elements.","Then the ratio of one element's mass (per fixed mass of the other) is a small whole-number ratio.","Look for a pair like CO/CO₂."]],
["m2-a-019",2,2,4,"Exam","mcq","Copper forms two oxides. Oxide I is 88.8% Cu and 11.2% O by mass; oxide II is 79.9% Cu and 20.1% O. What is the ratio of the mass of oxygen per gram of copper in oxide II to that in oxide I?",["1.79 : 1","1 : 2","2 : 1","1.11 : 1","3 : 2"],2,"Take 100 g of each oxide. Oxide I: 0.1261 g O per g Cu (11.2/88.8). Oxide II: 0.2516 g O per g Cu (20.1/79.9). Ratio II : I = 1.995 ≈ 2 : 1 - a small whole-number ratio, as the law of multiple proportions requires (oxide I is Cu₂O, oxide II is CuO). Distractors: 1.79 : 1 compares the mass percents directly without fixing the mass of copper; 1 : 2 inverts the ratio; 1.11 : 1 compares the copper; 3 : 2 is a guess.",["Fix the mass of copper: compute grams of O per gram of Cu in each oxide.","Then divide oxide II's value by oxide I's.","You should get close to a whole number."]],
["m2-a-020",2,2,4,"Exam","mcq","Three oxides of nitrogen contain, per 1.00 g of nitrogen, 1.14 g (oxide A), 2.28 g (oxide B) and 2.85 g (oxide C) of oxygen. If oxide A is NO, what is the formula of oxide C?",["NO₃","NO₂","N₂O₃","N₅O₂","N₂O₅"],4,"For a fixed 1.00 g of N, the oxygen masses are in the ratio 1.14 : 2.28 : 2.85 = 1 : 2.00 : 2.50 = 2 : 4 : 5. If A (2 parts O) is NO, the O-to-N atom ratio is 1 for A, 2 for B (NO₂) and 2.5 for C - so C has 5 O atoms per 2 N atoms: N₂O₅. Distractors: NO₃ rounds 2.5 up; NO₂ is oxide B; N₂O₃ would be a ratio of 1.5; N₅O₂ inverts the ratio.",["Divide each oxygen mass by the smallest one.","C has 2.5 times as much O per N as A does.","O : N = 2.5 : 1 = 5 : 2."]],
["m2-a-021",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which of Dalton's postulates is still accepted WITHOUT modification?",["Atoms are indivisible and contain no smaller particles","All atoms of a given element are identical in mass","Atoms of one element can never be converted into atoms of another element by any process","Compounds form by the chemical combination of a specific ratio of atoms of different elements","Atoms of different elements always have different mass numbers"],3,"The postulate that compounds have fixed ratios of atoms still stands (it explains definite composition). Atoms are NOT indivisible (electrons, protons and neutrons); atoms of an element are NOT all identical in mass (isotopes); nuclear reactions DO convert one element into another; and different elements can share a mass number (⁴⁰Ar and ⁴⁰Ca).",["Think about what electrons, isotopes and nuclear reactions did to Dalton's ideas.","Isotopes broke \"identical in mass\".","Which postulate is about compounds?"]],
["m2-a-022",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","The discovery of ISOTOPES showed that which of Dalton's ideas needed to be modified?",["Atoms combine in fixed whole-number ratios","Atoms are rearranged, not created or destroyed, in chemical reactions","Compounds have fixed compositions","Matter consists of atoms","All atoms of an element are identical in mass"],4,"Isotopes are atoms of the same element (same Z) with different numbers of neutrons and therefore different masses - so atoms of an element are NOT all identical in mass. (Their chemical behavior is nearly identical, which is why Dalton's other ideas still work.)",["What differs between ³⁵Cl and ³⁷Cl?","Same element, different mass.","Dalton said atoms of one element have the same mass."]],
["m2-a-023",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Cathode rays were deflected by magnetic and electric fields, bent toward the positive plate, and were the same no matter what metal the cathode was made of. What was concluded?",["Atoms have a small, dense, positive nucleus","Electrons carry a charge of −1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C","Atoms of all elements contain negatively charged particles (electrons)","Atoms are indivisible","Cathode rays are a beam of neutral atoms"],2,"Bending in fields → charged particles; bending toward the + plate → negative; identical for every cathode material → found in all matter. These particles (electrons) showed atoms are NOT indivisible. The nucleus came from Rutherford, and the electron's charge from Millikan.",["Match each observation to a conclusion.","Attracted to the positive plate means what charge?","Same for every metal → present in all matter."]],
["m2-a-024",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","What did Millikan's oil-drop experiment measure?",["the charge of the electron","the mass-to-charge ratio of the electron","the size of the nucleus","the mass of the proton","the number of neutrons in an atom"],0,"Millikan found that the charges on oil droplets were always whole-number multiples of one smallest charge, which he identified as the electron's charge (−1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C). Thomson had already measured the charge-to-mass ratio; combining the two gives the electron's mass.",["Thomson measured a ratio; Millikan measured one of its parts.","Charged droplets were suspended in an electric field.","Every droplet charge was a multiple of the same value."]],
["m2-a-025",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","In Rutherford's gold-foil experiment, MOST alpha particles passed straight through the foil. What did this show?",["The nucleus is negatively charged","An atom is mostly empty space","Electrons are much heavier than alpha particles","Gold atoms are packed with positive charge spread evenly throughout","Alpha particles have no charge"],1,"If most alpha particles meet nothing, most of each atom must be empty space (occupied only by light electrons). The rare large deflections are what revealed the tiny, dense, positive nucleus.",["What did the alpha particles encounter on their way through?","Nothing deflected them - so what is there?","Most of the atom's volume is ..."]],
["m2-a-026",2,2,3,"Concept","mcq","In the gold-foil experiment, a very few alpha particles (about 1 in 20,000) bounced back at large angles. Which conclusion follows from THIS observation?",["Electrons are spread evenly through a positive \"pudding\"","The atom is mostly empty space","Electrons orbit the nucleus in fixed circular paths","Each atom has a tiny, very dense, positively charged nucleus","The nucleus contains neutrons"],3,"Only a massive, concentrated positive charge could repel heavy, positive alpha particles back toward the source; that it happened so rarely means the target is tiny. \"Mostly empty space\" comes from the OTHER observation (most passed straight through). The plum-pudding model predicted no large deflections; neutrons were discovered later by Chadwick.",["Which observation is this one - the many or the few?","What could push a fast, heavy, positive particle backwards?","Small target + large repulsion."]],
["m2-a-027",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which atomic model did Rutherford's gold-foil results disprove?",["the nuclear model","Dalton's law of multiple proportions","the existence of electrons","Thomson's \"plum-pudding\" model","the law of mass conservation"],3,"Thomson pictured a diffuse positive sphere with electrons embedded in it. That model predicted almost no deflection of alpha particles; the large-angle scattering showed the positive charge is concentrated in a nucleus, which replaced the plum-pudding picture.",["What model was Rutherford testing?","It spread positive charge throughout the atom.","Raisins in a pudding."]],
["m2-a-028",2,2,4,"Exam","mcq","In an oil-drop experiment, four droplets carry charges of −6.40 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, −9.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, −16.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and −12.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ C. Assuming every charge is a whole-number multiple of one fundamental charge, what is the LARGEST value of that fundamental charge consistent with these data?",["−6.40 × 10⁻¹⁹ C","−1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C","−3.20 × 10⁻¹⁹ C","−11.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ C","−4.80 × 10⁻¹⁹ C"],2,"Divide each charge by trial values. ÷ 3.20: 6.40 → 2, 9.60 → 3, 16.0 → 5, 12.8 → 4 - all whole numbers. ÷ 6.40 gives 1.5 for 9.60 (not whole); ÷ 4.80 gives 1.33 for 6.40. 1.60 also divides evenly, but it is not the LARGEST such value. The average (11.2) has no meaning here. (With these invented numbers the \"unit\" is twice the real electron charge - Millikan's method finds the greatest common divisor of the data.)",["Each charge = n × (unit) with n a whole number.","Try dividing each value by the smallest one, then by smaller candidates.","6.40, 9.60, 12.8 and 16.0 are 2, 3, 4 and 5 times the same number."]],
["m2-a-029",2,2,3,"Calculation","num","Thomson found the electron's charge-to-mass ratio to be −1.76 × 10⁸ C/g, and Millikan measured its charge as −1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C. What is the mass of an electron in grams? (Enter a number in g.)",["9.10 × 10⁻²⁸ g"],9.102272727272728e-28,"mass = charge ÷ (charge/mass) = (−1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C) ÷ (−1.76 × 10⁸ C/g) = 9.10 × 10⁻²⁸ g (three significant figures from 1.76). Trap: multiplying the two values gives 2.82 × 10⁻¹¹, which has units of C²/g, not grams.",["Let the units guide you: C ÷ (C/g) = g.","mass = charge ÷ (charge/mass).","1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 1.76 × 10⁸."]],
["m2-a-030",2,2,1,"Concept","mcq","Which statement about the neutron is correct?",["It has a −1 charge and is found outside the nucleus","It has no charge and is found in the nucleus","It has a +1 charge and is found in the nucleus","It has no charge and orbits outside the nucleus","It has about 1/1836 the mass of a proton"],1,"Neutron: relative charge 0, mass about 1.00866 amu (very slightly more than a proton), located in the nucleus. The electron is the −1 particle outside the nucleus; the proton is +1 in the nucleus; the electron has about 1/1836 the mass of a proton.",["Neutral → charge?","Protons and neutrons together make up the nucleus.","Neutron: 0 charge, ~1 amu, nucleus."]],
["m2-a-031",2,2,1,"Concept","mcq","Which subatomic particle has by far the SMALLEST mass?",["electron","proton","neutron","alpha particle","hydrogen nucleus"],0,"The electron's mass is 0.00054858 amu (9.109 × 10⁻²⁸ g), about 1/1836 of a proton's. Protons and neutrons are each about 1 amu; an alpha particle (2 p + 2 n) is about 4 amu; a hydrogen nucleus is a single proton.",["Compare relative masses in amu.","Protons and neutrons are about 1 amu each.","The particle about 2000 times lighter than a proton."]],
["m2-a-032",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","A proton is approximately how many times more massive than an electron?",["about the same mass","about 2 times","about 100 times","about 1 million times","about 1800 times"],4,"Proton 1.00727 amu ÷ electron 0.00054858 amu ≈ 1836. That is why essentially all of an atom's mass is in its nucleus (99.97%) and why electrons are ignored when calculating mass numbers.",["Electron mass ≈ 0.00055 amu; proton mass ≈ 1.007 amu.","Divide the two.","Roughly two thousand."]],
["m2-a-033",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which statement about the nucleus is correct?",["It contains most of the atom's volume but little of its mass","It contains the electrons and protons","It is negatively charged","It contains nearly all of the atom's mass but only a tiny fraction of its volume","Its diameter is about half the diameter of the atom"],3,"The nucleus holds the protons and neutrons - about 99.97% of the mass - yet an atom's diameter (~10⁻¹⁰ m) is roughly 20,000 times the nucleus's (~5 × 10⁻¹⁵ m). It is positively charged, and electrons occupy the space around it.",["Where are the heavy particles?","Rutherford: most alpha particles passed straight through.","Huge mass, tiny size."]],
["m2-a-034",2,2,1,"Concept","mcq","The atomic number (Z) of an element is equal to the number of:",["neutrons in the nucleus","protons in the nucleus","protons plus neutrons","electrons in any ion of the element","isotopes of the element"],1,"Z = number of protons, and it defines the element: every carbon atom has Z = 6. In a NEUTRAL atom the number of electrons also equals Z, but in an ion it does not. Protons + neutrons is the mass number A.",["What makes carbon carbon?","Electrons change in ions, so they cannot define the element.","Z counts one kind of nuclear particle."]],
["m2-a-035",2,2,1,"Concept","mcq","The mass number (A) of an atom equals:",["the number of protons plus the number of neutrons","the number of protons only","the number of neutrons only","the average atomic mass from the periodic table","the number of protons plus electrons"],0,"A = Z + N, the total number of nucleons. It is a whole number, unlike the average atomic mass on the periodic table (e.g., 35.45 for Cl), which is a weighted average of isotopic masses.",["It counts the heavy particles.","Electrons are too light to count toward mass number.","A = Z + N."]],
["m2-a-036",2,2,2,"Calculation","mcq","How many neutrons are in an atom of chlorine-37 (³⁷Cl)?",["17","37","20","54","18"],2,"Chlorine has Z = 17. N = A − Z = 37 − 17 = 20. Distractors: 17 is the number of protons; 37 is the mass number; 54 adds A and Z; 18 is the neutron count of chlorine-35.",["Look up chlorine's atomic number.","N = A − Z.","37 − 17."]],
["m2-a-037",2,2,2,"Calculation","mcq","How many protons, neutrons and electrons are in the ion ⁸¹Br⁻?",["35 p, 46 n, 34 e⁻","35 p, 46 n, 35 e⁻","35 p, 81 n, 36 e⁻","46 p, 35 n, 36 e⁻","35 p, 46 n, 36 e⁻"],4,"Br: Z = 35, so 35 protons. N = 81 − 35 = 46 neutrons. A 1− charge means one EXTRA electron: 35 + 1 = 36 electrons. Distractors: 34 e⁻ removes an electron (the sign of the charge reversed); 35 e⁻ ignores the charge; 81 n uses the mass number; swapping p and n confuses Z with N.",["Z from the periodic table gives protons.","Neutrons = A − Z.","A negative charge means MORE electrons than protons."]],
["m2-a-038",2,2,2,"Calculation","num","How many electrons are in the ion ⁵⁶Fe³⁺? (Enter a whole number.)",["23"],23,"Fe has Z = 26, so a neutral atom has 26 electrons. A 3+ charge means 3 electrons have been REMOVED: 26 − 3 = 23 electrons. (The ion has 26 protons and 56 − 26 = 30 neutrons.) Trap: adding 3 gives 29.",["Start with the neutral atom: electrons = Z.","A positive charge means electrons were lost.","26 electrons minus 3 lost electrons."],{"abs":0.4}],
["m2-a-039",2,2,2,"Calculation","mcq","How many protons, neutrons and electrons are in ³²S²⁻?",["16 p, 16 n, 14 e⁻","16 p, 16 n, 16 e⁻","18 p, 14 n, 18 e⁻","16 p, 16 n, 18 e⁻","16 p, 32 n, 18 e⁻"],3,"S: Z = 16 → 16 protons. N = 32 − 16 = 16 neutrons. 2− charge → two extra electrons: 16 + 2 = 18 e⁻ (the same as argon). Distractors: 14 e⁻ subtracts instead of adding; 16 e⁻ ignores the charge; 18 p changes the element; 32 n uses the mass number.",["Protons come from Z.","Neutrons = A − Z.","A 2− ion has two more electrons than protons."]],
["m2-a-040",2,2,2,"Calculation","mcq","How many protons, neutrons and electrons are in ²⁷Al³⁺?",["13 p, 14 n, 10 e⁻","13 p, 14 n, 16 e⁻","13 p, 14 n, 13 e⁻","13 p, 27 n, 10 e⁻","10 p, 14 n, 13 e⁻"],0,"Al: Z = 13 → 13 protons. N = 27 − 13 = 14. 3+ charge → three electrons lost: 13 − 3 = 10 e⁻ (isoelectronic with Ne). Distractors: 16 e⁻ adds the charge; 13 e⁻ ignores it; 27 n uses A; the last one swaps protons and electrons.",["Protons = Z.","Neutrons = A − Z.","Cation: electrons = Z − charge."]],
["m2-a-041",2,2,2,"Calculation","mcq","How many protons, neutrons and electrons are in ¹³⁷Ba²⁺?",["56 p, 81 n, 58 e⁻","56 p, 81 n, 54 e⁻","56 p, 137 n, 54 e⁻","54 p, 83 n, 54 e⁻","56 p, 81 n, 56 e⁻"],1,"Ba: Z = 56. N = 137 − 56 = 81. Ba²⁺ has lost 2 electrons: 56 − 2 = 54 (isoelectronic with Xe). Distractors: 58 e⁻ adds the charge; 137 n uses A; 54 p changes the element (the charge never changes the number of protons); 56 e⁻ ignores the charge.",["The charge only changes the electrons.","N = A − Z = 137 − 56.","A 2+ ion has two fewer electrons than protons."]],
["m2-a-042",2,2,2,"Calculation","num","How many neutrons are in an atom of lead-207 (²⁰⁷Pb)? (Enter a whole number.)",["125"],125,"Pb has Z = 82. N = A − Z = 207 − 82 = 125 neutrons. Trap: 207.2 is the average atomic mass of lead, not a mass number - the mass number of this isotope is given as 207.",["Find lead's atomic number on the periodic table.","N = A − Z.","207 − 82."],{"abs":0.4}],
["m2-a-043",2,2,3,"Concept","mcq","Which species has 34 protons, 45 neutrons and 36 electrons?",["⁷⁹Br⁻","⁴⁵Se²⁻","⁷⁹Kr","⁷⁹Se²⁺","⁷⁹Se²⁻"],4,"Protons fix the element: Z = 34 is selenium. A = 34 + 45 = 79. Charge = protons − electrons = 34 − 36 = −2. So the species is ⁷⁹Se²⁻. Distractors: Br (Z = 35) and Kr (Z = 36) have the wrong number of protons (Kr uses the electron count); ⁴⁵Se uses N as the mass number; Se²⁺ gets the sign of the charge backwards.",["Which element has Z = 34?","Mass number = protons + neutrons.","Charge = protons − electrons."]],
["m2-a-044",2,2,3,"Concept","mcq","Which species has 26 protons, 30 neutrons and 24 electrons?",["⁵⁶Fe²⁻","³⁰Fe²⁺","⁵⁶Fe²⁺","⁵⁶Cr","⁵⁴Cr²⁺"],2,"Z = 26 is iron; A = 26 + 30 = 56; charge = 26 − 24 = +2 → ⁵⁶Fe²⁺. Distractors: Fe²⁻ reverses the sign; ³⁰Fe uses N as A; ⁵⁶Cr identifies the element from the electron count (Cr has Z = 24); ⁵⁴Cr²⁺ takes Z from the electron count (Cr) and adds the neutrons to it.",["Protons → element.","A = p + n.","Fewer electrons than protons → positive ion."]],
["m2-a-045",2,2,3,"Concept","mcq","Which pair are ISOTOPES of the same element?",["¹⁴C and ¹⁴N","⁴⁰Ar and ⁴⁰Ca","O₂ and O₃","²³⁵U and ²³⁸U","³⁹K and ⁴⁰Ca"],3,"Isotopes have the same atomic number (same element) and different numbers of neutrons. ²³⁵U and ²³⁸U both have Z = 92 (with 143 and 146 neutrons). ¹⁴C/¹⁴N and ⁴⁰Ar/⁴⁰Ca share a mass number but are different elements; ³⁹K and ⁴⁰Ca share a neutron count (20) but are different elements; O₂ and O₃ are molecules (allotropes), not isotopes.",["Isotopes = same element.","The left superscript (A) can differ; the element symbol cannot.","Same Z, different N."]],
["m2-a-046",2,2,3,"Concept","mcq","Which pair of atoms contains the SAME number of neutrons?",["¹⁴C and ¹⁶O","¹²C and ¹⁴N","³⁵Cl and ³⁷Cl","⁴⁰Ar and ⁴⁰K","²³Na and ²³Mg"],0,"N = A − Z. ¹⁴C: 14 − 6 = 8; ¹⁶O: 16 − 8 = 8 → equal. Others: ¹²C 6 vs ¹⁴N 7; ³⁵Cl 18 vs ³⁷Cl 20 (isotopes differ in neutrons by definition); ⁴⁰Ar 22 vs ⁴⁰K 21; ²³Na 12 vs ²³Mg 11. Trap: the same mass number does NOT mean the same number of neutrons.",["Work out N = A − Z for every atom.","Same A with different Z gives different N.","Carbon-14 has 8 neutrons - which oxygen isotope also has 8?"]],
["m2-a-047",2,2,3,"Concept","mcq","Which species is ISOELECTRONIC with (has the same number of electrons as) a neon atom?",["Na","Ca²⁺","Mg²⁺","Cl⁻","F"],2,"Neon has 10 electrons. Mg²⁺: 12 − 2 = 10 ✓. Na has 11; Ca²⁺ has 18 (like Ar); Cl⁻ has 18 (like Ar); F has 9 (F⁻ would have 10).",["Ne has Z = 10.","Electrons in an ion = Z − charge.","Which ion has 10 electrons?"]],
["m2-a-048",2,2,4,"Trap","mcq","Which species has MORE electrons than protons AND more neutrons than electrons?",["³⁵Cl⁻","¹⁹F⁻","³²S²⁻","⁴⁰K⁺","³⁷Cl⁻"],4,"Tabulate p, n, e⁻: ³⁷Cl⁻ 17, 20, 18 → e > p and n > e ✓. ³⁵Cl⁻ 17, 18, 18 (n = e). ¹⁹F⁻ 9, 10, 10 (n = e). ³²S²⁻ 16, 16, 18 (n < e). ⁴⁰K⁺ 19, 21, 18 (fewer electrons than protons). Only ³⁷Cl⁻ satisfies both conditions.",["Anions have more electrons than protons - rule out the cation.","For each anion compute N = A − Z and e⁻ = Z − charge.","Look for n strictly greater than e."]],
["m2-a-049",2,2,3,"Calculation","mcq","An ion has a 2− charge, 18 electrons and a mass number of 34. How many neutrons does it have?",["16","18","20","34","52"],1,"Protons = electrons + charge on an anion: 18 − 2 = 16 → the element is sulfur (Z = 16). N = A − Z = 34 − 16 = 18. Distractors: 16 is the number of protons; 20 adds the charge instead of subtracting it (Z = 20 would be Ca); 34 is A; 52 adds A and the electron count.",["First find the number of protons from the electrons and the charge.","A 2− ion has 2 more electrons than protons.","Z = 16; then N = 34 − Z."]],
["m2-a-050",2,2,3,"Calculation","mcq","An atom has a mass number of 88 and contains 50 neutrons. Identify the element, and give the number of neutrons in its isotope with mass number 86.",["strontium (Sr); 50 neutrons","strontium (Sr); 48 neutrons","tin (Sn); 36 neutrons","strontium (Sr); 38 neutrons","yttrium (Y); 47 neutrons"],1,"Z = A − N = 88 − 50 = 38 → strontium. Isotopes have the same Z, so the A = 86 isotope has N = 86 − 38 = 48 neutrons. Distractors: 50 assumes neutrons stay fixed (they are what changes between isotopes); tin (Z = 50) uses N as Z; 38 is the proton count; yttrium is Z = 39.",["Z = A − N.","Isotopes share Z but differ in N.","Z = 38; N = 86 − 38."]],
["m2-a-051",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which symbol represents a neutral atom with 17 protons and 20 neutrons?",["²⁰₁₇Cl","³⁷₂₀Ca","³⁷₁₇Cl⁻","¹⁷₃₇Cl","³⁷₁₇Cl"],4,"In ᴬ_Z X notation the left superscript is the mass number A = 17 + 20 = 37 and the left subscript is Z = 17 (chlorine): ³⁷₁₇Cl. Distractors: ²⁰₁₇Cl uses N as A; ³⁷₂₀Ca uses N as Z; ³⁷₁₇Cl⁻ is an ion (18 electrons), not a neutral atom; ¹⁷₃₇Cl swaps A and Z.",["Top left = mass number, bottom left = atomic number.","A = protons + neutrons.","Z = 17 is chlorine; A = 37."]],
["m2-a-052",2,2,4,"Trap","mcq","Which statement about ⁴⁰K⁺ and ⁴⁰Ca²⁺ is FALSE?",["They are isotopes of each other","They have the same number of electrons","They have the same mass number","They have different numbers of protons","⁴⁰K⁺ has one more neutron than ⁴⁰Ca²⁺"],0,"⁴⁰K⁺: 19 p, 21 n, 18 e⁻. ⁴⁰Ca²⁺: 20 p, 20 n, 18 e⁻. Same electrons (18) ✓, same A (40) ✓, different protons ✓, K⁺ has 21 vs 20 neutrons ✓. They are NOT isotopes - isotopes must be the same element (same Z). Trap: equal mass numbers or equal electron counts do not make two species isotopes.",["List p, n and e⁻ for each ion.","Isotopes must have the same number of protons.","K has Z = 19 and Ca has Z = 20."]],
["m2-a-053",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Why do the isotopes of an element have nearly identical chemical behavior?",["They have the same number of neutrons","They have the same mass","They have the same mass number","They have the same number of protons and therefore the same number of electrons","Their nuclei are all equally stable"],3,"Chemical behavior is governed by electrons, and the number of electrons in a neutral atom equals the number of protons, which is the same for all isotopes of an element. Isotopes differ in neutrons and therefore in mass, which barely affects chemistry.",["What do isotopes have in common?","Which particles take part in chemical reactions?","Same Z → same number of electrons."]],
["m2-a-054",2,2,1,"Concept","mcq","One atomic mass unit (amu, or dalton) is defined as:",["the mass of one hydrogen atom","the mass of one proton","exactly 1/12 the mass of one carbon-12 atom","the mass of one neutron","1/16 the mass of one oxygen atom"],2,"The modern standard is carbon-12, assigned exactly 12 amu; 1 amu = 1/12 of its mass = 1.66054 × 10⁻²⁴ g. A ¹H atom has a mass of 1.008 amu, and a proton 1.00727 amu, so neither is exactly 1 amu.",["The standard is a specific carbon isotope.","That isotope is assigned exactly 12 amu.","So 1 amu is one twelfth of it."]],
["m2-a-055",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","In a mass spectrometer, positive ions of different isotopes pass through a magnetic field. Which ions are deflected the MOST?",["the heaviest ions (highest mass/charge ratio)","all ions are deflected equally","the lightest ions (lowest mass/charge ratio)","only neutral atoms are deflected","the ions of the most abundant isotope"],2,"A mass spectrometer separates particles by their mass-to-charge ratio. For the same charge, the lightest particles have the least momentum and bend the most; the heaviest bend least. The detector records each isotope's position and relative abundance.",["Think of a heavy truck vs a bicycle turning a corner.","Same charge, different masses.","Lowest m/e bends most."]],
["m2-a-056",2,2,2,"Calculation","mcq","Copper consists of ⁶³Cu (62.9296 amu, 69.15%) and ⁶⁵Cu (64.9278 amu, 30.85%). What is the average atomic mass of copper?",["63.55 amu","63.93 amu","64.31 amu","64.00 amu","63.62 amu"],0,"Average atomic mass = Σ(isotopic mass × fractional abundance) = 62.9296(0.6915) + 64.9278(0.3085) = 43.516 + 20.030 = 63.55 amu. It lies closer to 63 because ⁶³Cu is more abundant. Distractors: 63.93 is an unweighted average; 64.31 swaps the abundances; 64.00 averages the mass numbers; 63.62 weights the mass numbers instead of the isotopic masses.",["Convert percents to fractions (÷ 100).","Multiply each isotopic mass by its own fraction, then add.","The answer must be closer to the more abundant isotope."]],
["m2-a-057",2,2,3,"Calculation","mcq","Silicon has three naturally occurring isotopes: ²⁸Si (27.9769 amu, 92.23%), ²⁹Si (28.9765 amu, 4.67%) and ³⁰Si (29.9738 amu, 3.10%). What is the average atomic mass of silicon?",["28.98 amu","28.09 amu","29.87 amu","27.16 amu","29.00 amu"],1,"27.9769(0.9223) + 28.9765(0.0467) + 29.9738(0.0310) = 25.803 + 1.353 + 0.929 = 28.09 amu. Distractors: 28.98 is unweighted; 29.87 gives the big abundance to the wrong isotope; 27.16 leaves out ³⁰Si; 29.00 averages the mass numbers.",["Sum (mass × fractional abundance) over all three isotopes.","92.23% → 0.9223.","Check: the answer should be just above 28."]],
["m2-a-058",2,2,3,"Calculation","num","Magnesium consists of ²⁴Mg (23.9850 amu, 78.99%), ²⁵Mg (24.9858 amu, 10.00%) and ²⁶Mg (25.9826 amu, 11.01%). Calculate the average atomic mass of magnesium. (Enter a number in amu, to two decimal places.)",["24.31 amu"],24.31,"23.9850(0.7899) + 24.9858(0.1000) + 25.9826(0.1101) = 18.9458 + 2.4986 + 2.8607 = 24.3050 → 24.31 amu, matching the periodic table. Trap: averaging the three masses without weights gives 24.98 amu.",["Change each percent into a decimal fraction.","Multiply and add the three contributions.","The result should be close to 24 because ²⁴Mg dominates."],{"abs":0.01}],
["m2-a-059",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Lithium has two isotopes, ⁶Li (6.015 amu) and ⁷Li (7.016 amu), and an average atomic mass of 6.94 amu. Which statement is correct?",["⁶Li is much more abundant than ⁷Li","The two isotopes are equally abundant","Some lithium atoms have a mass of exactly 6.94 amu","⁷Li is much more abundant than ⁶Li","The data are impossible because 6.94 is not a whole number"],3,"The average (6.94) lies much closer to 7.016 than to 6.015, so ⁷Li must dominate (about 92%). No individual atom has the average mass, and an average of isotopic masses is not expected to be a whole number.",["The weighted average is pulled toward the more abundant isotope.","Is 6.94 nearer to 6.015 or 7.016?","Equal abundance would give about 6.52."]],
["m2-a-060",2,2,4,"Calculation","num","Gallium (atomic mass 69.723 amu) has two isotopes: ⁶⁹Ga (68.9256 amu) and ⁷¹Ga (70.9247 amu). What is the percent abundance of ⁶⁹Ga? (Enter a number in %.)",["60.1%"],60.11,"Let x = fractional abundance of ⁶⁹Ga, so ⁷¹Ga is 1 − x. 68.9256x + 70.9247(1 − x) = 69.723 → 70.9247 − 1.9991x = 69.723 → x = (70.9247 − 69.723) ÷ 1.9991 = 0.6011 → 60.1% ⁶⁹Ga (and 39.9% ⁷¹Ga). Check: the average is closer to 69, so ⁶⁹Ga should be the majority. Trap: solving for the wrong isotope gives 39.9%.",["The two fractional abundances add up to 1.","Write mass₁·x + mass₂·(1 − x) = average and solve for x.","x = (mass₂ − average) ÷ (mass₂ − mass₁)."],{"tol":0.005}],
["m2-a-061",2,2,4,"Exam","mcq","Chlorine (atomic mass 35.45 amu) consists of ³⁵Cl (34.9689 amu) and ³⁷Cl (36.9659 amu). What are the natural abundances?",["24.1% ³⁵Cl and 75.9% ³⁷Cl","50.0% ³⁵Cl and 50.0% ³⁷Cl","77.5% ³⁵Cl and 22.5% ³⁷Cl","95.9% ³⁵Cl and 4.10% ³⁷Cl","75.9% ³⁵Cl and 24.1% ³⁷Cl"],4,"Let x = fraction of ³⁵Cl: 34.9689x + 36.9659(1 − x) = 35.45 → x = (36.9659 − 35.45) ÷ (36.9659 − 34.9689) = 1.5159 ÷ 1.9970 = 0.7591. So 75.9% ³⁵Cl and 24.1% ³⁷Cl. Distractors: swapping the two (the average would then be near 36.5); 50/50 (average 35.97); using mass numbers 35 and 37 instead of isotopic masses gives 77.5%; dividing 35.45 by 36.9659 is not an abundance calculation.",["Fractions add to 1: x and 1 − x.","Solve mass₁·x + mass₂(1 − x) = 35.45.","35.45 is closer to 35, so ³⁵Cl is the majority."]],
["m2-a-062",2,2,4,"Calculation","mcq","Bromine (atomic mass 79.90 amu) has two isotopes, ⁷⁹Br (78.9183 amu) and ⁸¹Br (80.9163 amu). What is the percent abundance of ⁷⁹Br?",["50.9%","49.1%","50.0%","55.0%","98.7%"],0,"x(78.9183) + (1 − x)(80.9163) = 79.90 → x = (80.9163 − 79.90) ÷ (80.9163 − 78.9183) = 1.0163 ÷ 1.9980 = 0.5087 → 50.9% ⁷⁹Br. The average is almost exactly midway, so the isotopes are nearly 50 : 50 - but not quite. Distractors: 49.1% is ⁸¹Br; 50.0% assumes equal amounts; 55.0% uses mass numbers; 98.7% divides the average by an isotopic mass.",["Set up the weighted-average equation with x and 1 − x.","Use the isotopic masses, not the mass numbers.","x = (80.9163 − 79.90) ÷ 1.9980."]],
["m2-a-063",2,2,4,"Calculation","num","Magnesium has an average atomic mass of 24.305 amu. ²⁴Mg (23.9850 amu) is 78.99% abundant and ²⁵Mg (24.9858 amu) is 10.00% abundant; the rest is ²⁶Mg. What is the isotopic mass of ²⁶Mg? (Enter a number in amu.)",["25.98 amu"],25.98,"Abundance of ²⁶Mg = 100.00 − 78.99 − 10.00 = 11.01%. 24.305 = 23.9850(0.7899) + 24.9858(0.1000) + m(0.1101) = 18.9458 + 2.4986 + 0.1101m. So 0.1101m = 24.305 − 21.4443 = 2.8607, and m = 25.98 amu. Check: it should be just under 26. Trap: forgetting to find the third abundance (or using 100% for it) gives a meaningless mass.",["The three abundances add to 100%.","Write the full weighted-average equation with the unknown mass.","Subtract the two known contributions, then divide by 0.1101."],{"abs":0.02}],
["m2-a-064",2,2,1,"Concept","mcq","The elements in Group 17 (F, Cl, Br, I) are called the:",["alkali metals","noble gases","halogens","alkaline earth metals","chalcogens"],2,"Group 17 = halogens (reactive nonmetals that form 1− ions). Group 1 = alkali metals, Group 2 = alkaline earth metals, Group 16 = chalcogens (oxygen family), Group 18 = noble gases.",["Second-to-last column.","They form salts with metals (the name means \"salt-former\").","F, Cl, Br, I."]],
["m2-a-065",2,2,1,"Concept","mcq","The horizontal rows of the periodic table are called:",["groups","families","blocks","series of isotopes","periods"],4,"Horizontal rows are periods; vertical columns are groups (also called families). Elements in the same group have similar chemical behavior.",["Columns are groups.","The other direction has a different name.","Period 3 runs from Na to Ar."]],
["m2-a-066",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which element is an ALKALINE EARTH metal?",["sodium (Na)","calcium (Ca)","aluminum (Al)","chlorine (Cl)","iron (Fe)"],1,"Alkaline earth metals are Group 2: Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba, Ra. Sodium is an alkali metal (Group 1); aluminum is in Group 13; chlorine is a halogen; iron is a transition metal.",["Alkaline earth metals form 2+ ions.","They are in Group 2.","Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba."]],
["m2-a-067",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which element is a METALLOID?",["aluminum (Al)","selenium (Se)","tin (Sn)","arsenic (As)","phosphorus (P)"],3,"The metalloids lie along the staircase line: B, Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te. Aluminum and tin are metals; selenium and phosphorus are nonmetals.",["Metalloids sit along the stair-step line between metals and nonmetals.","The list is B, Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te.","Which choice is on that list?"]],
["m2-a-068",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which element is a TRANSITION metal?",["magnesium (Mg)","gallium (Ga)","manganese (Mn)","calcium (Ca)","antimony (Sb)"],2,"Transition metals fill the middle block, Groups 3-12 (Sc to Zn in period 4). Mn is in Group 7. Mg and Ca are Group 2 metals; Ga is a Group 13 main-group metal; Sb is a metalloid. Trap: Mn and Mg look alike.",["Transition metals are in Groups 3 to 12.","Find each element's group number.","Do not confuse Mg with Mn."]],
["m2-a-069",2,2,3,"Concept","mcq","Which description fits the element with atomic number Z = 34?",["selenium - a nonmetal in Group 16, period 4","selenium - a metal in Group 6, period 4","bromine - a halogen in Group 17, period 4","arsenic - a metalloid in Group 15, period 4","strontium - an alkaline earth metal in Group 2, period 5"],0,"Z = 34 is selenium (Se), in period 4 and Group 16 (same group as O and S), and it is a nonmetal. Bromine is 35, arsenic 33, strontium 38. \"Group 6\" confuses the old 6A label with the transition-metal Group 6 (Cr).",["Find Z = 34 on the periodic table.","It sits just below sulfur.","Group 16, period 4."]],
["m2-a-070",2,2,3,"Concept","mcq","Which element is in period 4, Group 15, and what kind of element is it?",["phosphorus, a nonmetal","arsenic, a metalloid","antimony, a metalloid","selenium, a nonmetal","vanadium, a transition metal"],1,"Period 4, Group 15 is arsenic (As, Z = 33), one of the metalloids. Phosphorus is period 3; antimony is period 5; selenium is Group 16; vanadium is Group 5 (a transition metal) in period 4.",["Period = row, Group = column.","Group 15 is the nitrogen family.","Go down from N and P to period 4."]],
["m2-a-071",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which property is typical of NONMETALS?",["they are shiny and conduct electricity well","they tend to lose electrons to form cations","they are malleable and ductile","they tend to gain electrons to form anions","they are all gases at room temperature"],3,"Nonmetals (upper right of the table) tend to gain electrons and form anions (O²⁻, Cl⁻). Shine, conductivity, malleability and forming cations are metal properties. Nonmetals include solids (C, S, P, I) and a liquid (Br₂), not only gases.",["Metals vs nonmetals: which side gains electrons?","Think of chlorine becoming chloride.","Gaining electrons gives a negative ion."]],
["m2-a-072",2,2,3,"Concept","mcq","Which set contains ONLY metals?",["Na, Si, Fe","K, Ca, Br","B, Al, Ga","Cu, As, Zn","Na, Mg, Fe"],4,"Na (alkali metal), Mg (alkaline earth metal) and Fe (transition metal) are all metals. Si, B and As are metalloids; Br is a nonmetal.",["Check each element's position relative to the staircase.","B, Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te are metalloids.","One set has no metalloid or nonmetal."]],
["m2-a-073",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which element would you expect to be most similar chemically to sulfur?",["phosphorus (P)","selenium (Se)","chlorine (Cl)","silicon (Si)","argon (Ar)"],1,"Elements in the same GROUP have similar chemical properties. Selenium is directly below sulfur in Group 16 (both form 2− ions, e.g., H₂S and H₂Se). P, Cl, Si and Ar are sulfur's neighbors in the same PERIOD, which does not make them chemically similar.",["Similar chemistry comes from the same group, not the same period.","Sulfur is in Group 16.","Which choice is in Group 16?"]],
["m2-a-074",2,2,1,"Concept","mcq","What ion does calcium form?",["Ca²⁺","Ca⁺","Ca²⁻","Ca³⁺","Ca⁻"],0,"Calcium is in Group 2. It loses its two outer electrons to reach the electron count of argon (20 − 2 = 18), forming Ca²⁺. Metals form cations, never anions.",["Calcium is a metal in Group 2.","Group 2 metals lose two electrons.","Losing electrons gives a positive charge."]],
["m2-a-075",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","What monatomic ion does nitrogen form in ionic compounds such as Mg₃N₂?",["N³⁺","N⁵⁺","N³⁻","N⁻","N⁵⁻"],2,"Nitrogen is a Group 15 nonmetal. Main-group nonmetal anions have charge = group number − 18 = 15 − 18 = −3: N³⁻ (nitride), which has 10 electrons like neon. Check with Mg₃N₂: 3(+2) + 2(−3) = 0.",["Nonmetals gain electrons.","Anion charge = group number − 18.","15 − 18 = ?"]],
["m2-a-076",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","What monatomic ion does selenium form?",["Se²⁺","Se⁶⁺","Se⁻","Se⁶⁻","Se²⁻"],4,"Selenium is in Group 16 (below O and S): charge = 16 − 18 = −2, so selenide, Se²⁻, with 36 electrons like krypton.",["Selenium is in the oxygen/sulfur group.","Group 16 nonmetals gain two electrons.","Like O²⁻ and S²⁻."]],
["m2-a-077",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which ion is NOT one you would expect a main-group element to form?",["Al³⁺","S²⁻","I⁻","Na²⁺","Ba²⁺"],3,"Sodium (Group 1) loses just one electron to reach neon's electron count: Na⁺. Removing a second electron would mean removing an inner (core) electron, which is far too costly, so Na²⁺ does not form. Al³⁺ (Group 13), S²⁻ (Group 16), I⁻ (Group 17) and Ba²⁺ (Group 2) are all expected.",["Check each ion against its group.","Group 1 → 1+, Group 2 → 2+, Group 13 → 3+, Group 16 → 2−, Group 17 → 1−.","Which charge does not match its group?"]],
["m2-a-078",2,2,4,"Trap","mcq","A monatomic ion X²⁺ has 36 electrons. What is element X?",["krypton (Kr)","strontium (Sr)","selenium (Se)","rubidium (Rb)","zirconium (Zr)"],1,"A 2+ ion has 2 fewer electrons than protons, so protons = 36 + 2 = 38 → strontium (Group 2, which does form 2+ ions). Distractors: Kr (36) uses the electron count as Z; Se (34) subtracts the charge; Rb (37) adds only one; Zr is 40.",["Protons = electrons + charge for a cation.","36 + 2 = ?","Z = 38 - find it on the table."]],
["m2-a-079",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which pair of ions BOTH have the same number of electrons as an argon atom?",["Na⁺ and Cl⁻","K⁺ and F⁻","Li⁺ and Br⁻","Mg²⁺ and O²⁻","K⁺ and Cl⁻"],4,"Argon has 18 electrons. K⁺: 19 − 1 = 18; Cl⁻: 17 + 1 = 18. ✓ Na⁺ (10) and F⁻ (10) and Mg²⁺/O²⁻ (10 each) match neon; Li⁺ (2) matches helium and Br⁻ (36) matches krypton.",["Argon has 18 electrons.","Electrons in an ion = Z − charge.","Look for the period 3/4 neighbors of argon."]],
["m2-a-080",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which compound is IONIC?",["KBr","CO₂","NH₃","SCl₂","CH₄"],0,"Ionic compounds generally form between a metal and a nonmetal: potassium (Group 1 metal) transfers an electron to bromine, giving K⁺ and Br⁻. The others are combinations of nonmetals only, so they are covalent (molecular).",["Look for a metal combined with a nonmetal.","Which choice contains a metal?","Potassium is an alkali metal."]],
["m2-a-081",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which compound is MOLECULAR (covalent) rather than ionic?",["CaO","MgCl₂","N₂O₄","Na₂S","Al₂O₃"],2,"N₂O₄ contains only nonmetals (N and O), so it is made of discrete covalently bonded molecules. CaO, MgCl₂, Na₂S and Al₂O₃ each combine a metal with a nonmetal and are ionic.",["Covalent compounds usually contain only nonmetals.","Check each formula for a metal.","The one without a metal is molecular."]],
["m2-a-082",2,2,3,"Concept","mcq","Which substance contains BOTH ionic bonds and covalent bonds?",["NaCl","SO₂","H₂O","Na₂SO₄","CaF₂"],3,"In Na₂SO₄, Na⁺ ions are held to SO₄²⁻ ions by ionic bonding, while the S and O atoms inside the sulfate ion are held together by covalent bonds. NaCl and CaF₂ are purely ionic (monatomic ions); SO₂ and H₂O are purely covalent.",["Polyatomic ions are held together internally by covalent bonds.","Which compound contains a polyatomic ion?","Sulfate = SO₄²⁻."]],
["m2-a-083",2,2,2,"Concept","mcq","Which property is typical of an IONIC compound?",["It conducts electricity when melted or dissolved in water","It has a low melting point","It exists as discrete molecules","It forms when two nonmetals share electrons","It conducts electricity as a solid"],0,"Ionic solids are arrays of ions locked in place, so they do not conduct as solids, but when melted or dissolved the ions can move and carry current. They have high melting points and no discrete molecules. Sharing electrons between nonmetals describes covalent bonding.",["What carries current in a melted salt?","In the solid, the ions cannot move.","Mobile ions only when molten or dissolved."]],
["m2-a-084",2,2,3,"Concept","mcq","Why does magnesium form Mg²⁺ ions rather than Mg⁺ ions?",["Magnesium has two protons","Magnesium is in period 2","Losing two electrons gives Mg the same number of electrons as the noble gas neon","Losing two electrons gives Mg the same number of electrons as argon","Magnesium gains two electrons to complete its outer shell"],2,"Mg (Z = 12) loses its two outer electrons to reach 10 electrons, the same as neon, the nearest noble gas. It is in Group 2 and period 3 and has 12 protons. Metals lose electrons; they do not gain them.",["How many electrons does Mg have, and which noble gas is nearest?","Ne has 10 electrons.","12 − 2 = 10."]],
["m2-a-085",2,2,4,"Exam","mcq","Element X is in period 3 and forms an anion with 18 electrons and a 2− charge. Element Y is the Group 13 metal in period 3. What is the formula and name of the compound formed by X and Y?",["Al₃S₂, aluminum sulfide","AlS, aluminum sulfide","Al₂(SO₄)₃, aluminum sulfate","Al₂S₃, dialuminum trisulfide","Al₂S₃, aluminum sulfide"],4,"X: an anion with 18 e⁻ and 2− charge has 18 − 2 = 16 protons → S, forming S²⁻. Y: Group 13, period 3 → Al, forming Al³⁺. Neutral formula: 2 Al³⁺ (6+) balance 3 S²⁻ (6−) → Al₂S₃, aluminum sulfide. Distractors: Al₃S₂ swaps the subscripts; AlS ignores the charges; sulfate is SO₄²⁻, not S²⁻; ionic compounds never use numerical prefixes.",["Find X from electrons and charge; find Y from its position.","Al forms 3+, S forms 2−.","Cross the charges: Al₂S₃."]],
["m2-a-086",2,3,1,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of MgCl₂?",["magnesium dichloride","magnesium chloride","magnesium(II) chloride","manganese(II) chloride","magnesium chlorine"],1,"Binary ionic compound: metal name + nonmetal root + -ide. Magnesium forms only Mg²⁺, so no Roman numeral is used, and ionic names never use prefixes like di-. \"Manganese\" is Mn, a different element; \"chlorine\" is the element name, not the anion name.",["Cation name, then anion name ending in -ide.","Mg forms only one ion, so no Roman numeral.","No prefixes in ionic names."]],
["m2-a-087",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of aluminum oxide?",["AlO","Al₃O₂","AlO₃","Al₂O₃","Al₂O"],3,"Al³⁺ and O²⁻: the smallest whole numbers that balance charge are 2 Al³⁺ (6+) and 3 O²⁻ (6−) → Al₂O₃. Distractors either ignore the charges (AlO), swap them (Al₃O₂: 9+ and 4−), or leave the compound charged.",["Aluminum forms 3+; oxide is 2−.","Find the lowest common multiple of 3 and 2.","Total positive charge = total negative charge = 6."]],
["m2-a-088",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of magnesium nitride?",["Mg₂N₃","Mg₃N₂","MgN","Mg(NO₃)₂","Mg₃N"],1,"Mg²⁺ and N³⁻ (nitride, 15 − 18 = −3). Three Mg²⁺ (6+) balance two N³⁻ (6−): Mg₃N₂ (Silberberg Sample Problem 2.8). Mg(NO₃)₂ is magnesium NITRATE; the others are not charge-balanced.",["Nitride is the monatomic ion of nitrogen.","Mg²⁺ and N³⁻.","LCM of 2 and 3 is 6."]],
["m2-a-089",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of Cs₂S?",["cesium sulfate","dicesium sulfide","cesium(I) sulfide","cesium sulfite","cesium sulfide"],4,"Cs⁺ (Group 1) and S²⁻ (sulfide). Two Cs⁺ balance one S²⁻. Name: cesium sulfide - no prefix (ionic) and no Roman numeral (Cs forms only one ion). Sulfate (SO₄²⁻) and sulfite (SO₃²⁻) contain oxygen.",["Is there any oxygen in the formula?","S alone as an anion is sulfide.","Group 1 metals need no Roman numeral."]],
["m2-a-090",2,3,2,"Worksheet","mcq","What is the systematic name of Fe₂O₃ (Week 3 worksheet compound)?",["iron(III) oxide","iron(II) oxide","diiron trioxide","iron oxide","iron(III) trioxide"],0,"Oxide is O²⁻; three oxide ions carry 6−, so two Fe must carry 6+, i.e., each Fe is 3+. Fe forms more than one ion, so the charge goes in Roman numerals: iron(III) oxide. The numeral is the CHARGE, not the subscript. Prefixes (di-, tri-) are for molecular compounds only; \"iron oxide\" is ambiguous (FeO or Fe₂O₃).",["Find the charge on each Fe from the total charge of the oxide ions.","3 × (−2) = −6, shared by 2 Fe.","Fe³⁺ → iron(III)."]],
["m2-a-091",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of FeO?",["iron(I) oxide","iron(III) oxide","iron monoxide","iron(II) oxide","iron oxide(II)"],3,"One O²⁻ (2−) means one Fe must be 2+: iron(II) oxide (common name ferrous oxide). The Roman numeral goes right after the metal name. \"Monoxide\" is molecular naming, not ionic.",["Oxide is 2−.","One Fe balances one O²⁻.","Fe²⁺ → iron(II)."]],
["m2-a-092",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the systematic name of CrI₃?",["chromium triiodide","chromium(I) iodide","chromium(III) iodide","chromium iodide","chromium(III) iodine"],2,"Three I⁻ ions (3−) require Cr³⁺: chromium(III) iodide (Silberberg Sample Problem 2.9). Chromium forms more than one ion (Cr²⁺, Cr³⁺), so the numeral is required. No prefixes in ionic names; the anion is iodide, not iodine.",["Iodide is I⁻.","Three of them balance one Cr - what is Cr's charge?","Roman numeral = charge on Cr."]],
["m2-a-093",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the systematic name of CoS?",["cobalt(II) sulfide","cobalt(I) sulfide","cobalt sulfide","copper(II) sulfide","cobalt(II) sulfate"],0,"Sulfide is S²⁻, so Co is Co²⁺: cobalt(II) sulfide. Cobalt forms 2+ and 3+ ions, so the numeral is required. Co is cobalt, not copper (Cu); sulfate would be SO₄²⁻.",["S alone is sulfide, 2−.","One Co balances one S²⁻.","Co²⁺ → cobalt(II)."]],
["m2-a-094",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of lead(IV) oxide?",["Pb₄O","PbO","PbO₂","PbO₄","Pb₄O₂"],2,"lead(IV) = Pb⁴⁺; oxide = O²⁻. Two O²⁻ (4−) balance one Pb⁴⁺: PbO₂. Trap: the Roman numeral is the charge, not a subscript, so Pb₄O, PbO₄ and Pb₄O₂ all misuse it (none is charge-balanced with Pb⁴⁺ and O²⁻); PbO is lead(II) oxide.",["(IV) means a 4+ charge on lead.","Oxide is 2−.","How many O²⁻ balance 4+?"]],
["m2-a-095",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the systematic name of Cu₂S?",["copper(II) sulfide","dicopper sulfide","copper(I) sulfite","copper(I) sulfide","copper sulfide"],3,"S²⁻ (2−) is balanced by two Cu, so each Cu is 1+: copper(I) sulfide (cuprous sulfide). Trap: reading the subscript 2 as the charge gives copper(II). Sulfite is SO₃²⁻; prefixes are not used for ionic compounds; copper forms two ions, so the numeral is needed.",["Total negative charge = 2−, shared by two Cu.","Each Cu is +1.","The Roman numeral is the charge on ONE copper ion."]],
["m2-a-096",2,3,3,"Trap","mcq","What is the formula of iron(II) sulfate?",["Fe₂(SO₄)₃","Fe₂SO₄","Fe(SO₄)₂","FeSO₃","FeSO₄"],4,"Fe²⁺ and SO₄²⁻ balance one-to-one: FeSO₄ (Silberberg Sample Problem 2.11 corrects exactly this mistake). Fe₂(SO₄)₃ is iron(III) sulfate; Fe₂SO₄ treats the numeral as a subscript; Fe(SO₄)₂ would need Fe⁴⁺; FeSO₃ is iron(II) sulfite.",["Sulfate is SO₄²⁻.","Iron(II) is Fe²⁺.","2+ and 2− balance in a 1 : 1 ratio."]],
["m2-a-097",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of copper(II) nitride?",["Cu(NO₃)₂","Cu₃N₂","Cu₂N₃","CuN₂","Cu₂N"],1,"Cu²⁺ and N³⁻ (nitride). LCM of 2 and 3 is 6: three Cu²⁺ and two N³⁻ → Cu₃N₂. Cu(NO₃)₂ is copper(II) NITRATE (a common confusion); the others are not charge-balanced.",["Nitride (N³⁻) is not nitrate (NO₃⁻).","Copper(II) = Cu²⁺.","Balance 2+ against 3−."]],
["m2-a-098",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of tin(IV) chloride?",["SnCl₂","Sn₄Cl","SnCl₄","SnCl","TiCl₄"],2,"tin(IV) = Sn⁴⁺; four Cl⁻ balance it: SnCl₄. SnCl₂ is tin(II) chloride; Sn₄Cl misuses the numeral as a subscript; Ti is titanium, not tin.",["The symbol for tin is Sn (from Latin stannum).","(IV) is the charge, +4.","Four chloride ions."]],
["m2-a-099",2,3,1,"Concept","mcq","The common (trivial) name \"cuprous\" refers to which ion?",["Cu²⁺, copper(II)","Co²⁺, cobalt(II)","Cu³⁺, copper(III)","Cr³⁺, chromium(III)","Cu⁺, copper(I)"],4,"In common names, -ous is the LOWER charge and -ic the higher: cuprous = Cu⁺, cupric = Cu²⁺ (memory aid: the o in -ous and lower, the i in -ic and higher). \"Cupr-\" is the Latin root for copper.",["-ous vs -ic: which is lower?","Copper forms Cu⁺ and Cu²⁺.","o in -ous, o in lower."]],
["m2-a-100",2,3,1,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of the sulfite ion?",["SO₄²⁻","S²⁻","SO₃⁻","SO₃²⁻","HSO₃⁻"],3,"Sulfate is SO₄²⁻; sulfite has one fewer O and the same charge: SO₃²⁻. S²⁻ is sulfide; HSO₃⁻ is hydrogen sulfite; the charge of sulfite is 2−, not 1−.",["-ate has more oxygen than -ite.","Start from sulfate, SO₄²⁻.","Remove one O; keep the charge."]],
["m2-a-101",2,3,1,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of the ClO₂⁻ ion?",["chlorite","chlorate","hypochlorite","perchlorate","chloride"],0,"The chlorine oxoanion family: ClO₄⁻ perchlorate, ClO₃⁻ chlorate, ClO₂⁻ chlorite, ClO⁻ hypochlorite. Chloride is Cl⁻ with no oxygen.",["Recall the four oxoanions of chlorine.","Chlorate is ClO₃⁻.","One fewer O than chlorate → -ite."]],
["m2-a-102",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","Which formula-name pair is CORRECT?",["CrO₄²⁻ - dichromate","MnO₄⁻ - permanganate","NO₂⁻ - nitrate","HCO₃⁻ - carbonate","ClO₃⁻ - perchlorate"],1,"MnO₄⁻ is permanganate ✓. CrO₄²⁻ is chromate (dichromate is Cr₂O₇²⁻); NO₂⁻ is nitrite (nitrate is NO₃⁻); HCO₃⁻ is hydrogen carbonate (carbonate is CO₃²⁻); ClO₃⁻ is chlorate (perchlorate is ClO₄⁻).",["Check each against the polyatomic ion list.","Watch for -ate vs -ite, per-, di- and hydrogen.","Permanganate has four O and a 1− charge."]],
["m2-a-103",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the charge on the phosphate ion, PO₄?",["2−","1−","4−","3+","3−"],4,"Phosphate is PO₄³⁻ (sodium phosphate is Na₃PO₄). Hydrogen phosphate is HPO₄²⁻ and dihydrogen phosphate is H₂PO₄⁻ - each H⁺ added reduces the negative charge by one.",["Na₃PO₄ is sodium phosphate.","Three Na⁺ balance one phosphate.","So phosphate carries 3−."]],
["m2-a-104",2,3,2,"Worksheet","mcq","What is the name of Li₂SO₃ (Week 3 worksheet compound)?",["lithium sulfate","lithium sulfide","dilithium sulfite","lithium sulfite","lithium(I) sulfite"],3,"SO₃²⁻ is sulfite; two Li⁺ balance it. Name: lithium sulfite. Sulfate is SO₄²⁻ and sulfide is S²⁻. Ionic names never use prefixes (dilithium), and lithium forms only Li⁺, so no Roman numeral.",["Identify the polyatomic ion SO₃²⁻.","Sulfate has 4 O; sulfite has 3.","Group 1 metals need no numeral."]],
["m2-a-105",2,3,1,"Worksheet","mcq","What is the name of CaCO₃ (Week 3 worksheet compound)?",["calcium carbide","calcium carbonate","calcium hydrogen carbonate","calcium(II) carbonate","calcium carbonite"],1,"CO₃²⁻ is the carbonate ion; Ca²⁺ balances it 1 : 1. Name: calcium carbonate (limestone, chalk). Calcium forms only Ca²⁺, so no numeral; hydrogen carbonate is HCO₃⁻ (Ca(HCO₃)₂); \"carbonite\" is not an ion on the list.",["CO₃²⁻ is a polyatomic ion you must know.","Ca forms only one ion.","Metal name + polyatomic ion name."]],
["m2-a-106",2,3,2,"Worksheet","mcq","What is the formula of lithium sulfite?",["LiSO₃","Li₂SO₄","Li₂SO₃","Li₂S","Li(SO₃)₂"],2,"Li⁺ and SO₃²⁻: two Li⁺ balance one sulfite → Li₂SO₃. LiSO₃ is not neutral; Li₂SO₄ is lithium sulfate; Li₂S is lithium sulfide; Li(SO₃)₂ would need Li⁴⁺.",["Sulfite = SO₃²⁻.","Lithium = Li⁺.","Two Li⁺ per sulfite."]],
["m2-a-107",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of calcium nitrate?",["Ca(NO₃)₂","CaNO₃","Ca(NO₂)₂","Ca₂NO₃","Ca₃N₂"],0,"Ca²⁺ needs two NO₃⁻ ions; parentheses show that the whole nitrate ion is doubled: Ca(NO₃)₂. CaNO₃ is not neutral; Ca(NO₂)₂ is calcium nitrite; Ca₂NO₃ has the ratio backwards; Ca₃N₂ is calcium nitride.",["Nitrate = NO₃⁻.","Calcium = Ca²⁺.","Two nitrates - put the ion in parentheses."]],
["m2-a-108",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of ammonium phosphate?",["(NH₃)₄PO₄","(NH₄)₃PO₄","NH₄PO₄","(NH₄)₂PO₄","(NH₄)₃PO₃"],1,"Ammonium is NH₄⁺; phosphate is PO₄³⁻. Three NH₄⁺ balance one PO₄³⁻: (NH₄)₃PO₄. (NH₃)₄PO₄ confuses ammonia with ammonium (Silberberg Follow-up 2.11A); the next two are not neutral; PO₃³⁻ is phosphite.",["Both ions are polyatomic.","NH₄⁺ (1+) and PO₄³⁻ (3−).","Three ammonium ions in parentheses."]],
["m2-a-109",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of aluminum hydroxide?",["AlOH₃","AlOH","Al(OH)₃","Al(OH)₂","Al₃OH"],2,"Al³⁺ needs three OH⁻: Al(OH)₃. The parentheses matter: AlOH₃ would mean one O and three H, not three hydroxide ions. The others are not charge-balanced.",["Hydroxide is OH⁻.","Aluminum is 3+.","Three hydroxides need parentheses."]],
["m2-a-110",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the systematic name of Fe(ClO₄)₂?",["iron(II) perchlorate","iron(II) chlorate","iron diperchlorate","iron(III) perchlorate","iron(I) perchlorate"],0,"ClO₄⁻ is perchlorate (1−). Two of them (2−) mean Fe is 2+: iron(II) perchlorate (Silberberg Sample Problem 2.10). Chlorate is ClO₃⁻; no prefixes for ionic compounds; the numeral must equal the cation's charge.",["Identify the anion: ClO₄⁻.","Two of them give 2−.","Fe²⁺ → iron(II)."]],
["m2-a-111",2,3,3,"Trap","mcq","What is the name of Mg(HCO₃)₂?",["manganese(II) hydrogen carbonate","magnesium carbonate","magnesium(II) hydrogen carbonate","magnesium hydrogen carbonate","magnesium dihydrogen carbonate"],3,"Mg²⁺ with two HCO₃⁻ (hydrogen carbonate, also called bicarbonate): magnesium hydrogen carbonate. Mg is magnesium, not manganese (Mn) - Silberberg Follow-up 2.11A uses exactly this trap. Carbonate is CO₃²⁻ (MgCO₃); Mg needs no numeral; \"dihydrogen\" would wrongly describe the ion.",["Mg or Mn? Check the symbol.","HCO₃⁻ is hydrogen carbonate.","One Mg²⁺, two HCO₃⁻."]],
["m2-a-112",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of Ca(NO₂)₂?",["calcium nitrate","cadmium nitrite","calcium dinitrite","calcium nitride","calcium nitrite"],4,"NO₂⁻ is nitrite (one fewer O than nitrate, NO₃⁻). Ca is calcium (Cd is cadmium). Ionic names use no prefixes. Nitride is N³⁻ (no oxygen).",["Count the oxygens in the anion.","NO₃⁻ nitrate, NO₂⁻ nitrite.","Ca = calcium."]],
["m2-a-113",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of potassium dichromate?",["K₂CrO₄","KCr₂O₇","K(Cr₂O₇)₂","K₃Cr₂O₇","K₂Cr₂O₇"],4,"Dichromate is Cr₂O₇²⁻; two K⁺ balance it: K₂Cr₂O₇. K₂CrO₄ is potassium chromate (CrO₄²⁻); the other three are not charge-balanced.",["Dichromate = Cr₂O₇²⁻.","Potassium = K⁺.","Two K⁺ per dichromate."]],
["m2-a-114",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of sodium hypochlorite (household bleach)?",["NaClO","NaClO₂","NaClO₃","NaCl","Na(ClO)₂"],0,"Hypochlorite is ClO⁻ (the fewest oxygens in the chlorine family). Na⁺ balances it 1 : 1: NaClO. NaClO₂ is sodium chlorite, NaClO₃ sodium chlorate, NaCl sodium chloride.",["hypo- ... -ite = fewest oxygens.","The ion is ClO⁻.","Na⁺ and ClO⁻."]],
["m2-a-115",2,3,4,"Trap","mcq","Which name-formula pair is INCORRECT?",["Cu(NO₃)₂ - copper(II) nitrate","SnO₂ - tin(IV) oxide","Na₂CO₃ - sodium carbonate","Fe₂(SO₄)₃ - iron(II) sulfate","NH₄Cl - ammonium chloride"],3,"In Fe₂(SO₄)₃, three SO₄²⁻ carry 6−, so each of the two Fe is 3+: the name is iron(III) sulfate. The others check out: Cu²⁺ with two NO₃⁻; Sn⁴⁺ with two O²⁻; two Na⁺ with CO₃²⁻; NH₄⁺ with Cl⁻. Trap: reading the subscript 2 on Fe as the Roman numeral.",["For each transition-metal compound, compute the metal's charge.","Total anion charge ÷ number of metal ions.","3 × (−2) = −6 shared by 2 Fe."]],
["m2-a-116",2,3,4,"Trap","mcq","Which formula is WRONG for the name given?",["barium acetate - Ba(C₂H₃O₂)₂","sodium sulfide - Na₂S","aluminum hydroxide - AlOH₃","cesium carbonate - Cs₂CO₃","potassium chlorate - KClO₃"],2,"Aluminum hydroxide is Al(OH)₃ - three hydroxide ions need parentheses; AlOH₃ reads as one O and three H atoms. The others are correct: Ba²⁺ with two acetates; two Na⁺ with S²⁻; two Cs⁺ with one CO₃²⁻ (no parentheses for a single polyatomic ion); K⁺ with ClO₃⁻.",["Check charges AND the use of parentheses.","Parentheses are needed when there is more than one of a polyatomic ion.","How many OH⁻ does Al³⁺ need?"]],
["m2-a-117",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of MgSO₄·7H₂O (Epsom salt)?",["magnesium sulfate hexahydrate","magnesium sulfate heptahydrate","magnesium sulfite heptahydrate","magnesium(II) sulfate heptahydrate","magnesium heptasulfate hydrate"],1,"Name the ionic compound (magnesium sulfate, SO₄²⁻), then add a Greek prefix for the number of waters + \"hydrate\": 7 = hepta → magnesium sulfate heptahydrate. Hexa = 6; sulfite is SO₃²⁻; Mg needs no numeral.",["Name the salt first, then the water.","Greek prefixes: 6 hexa, 7 hepta, 8 octa.","Seven waters → heptahydrate."]],
["m2-a-118",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of copper(II) sulfate pentahydrate?",["CuSO₄·5H₂O","Cu₂SO₄·5H₂O","CuSO₄·6H₂O","CuSO₃·5H₂O","Cu(SO₄)₂·5H₂O"],0,"Copper(II) = Cu²⁺; sulfate = SO₄²⁻ → CuSO₄; penta = 5 waters → CuSO₄·5H₂O, the blue crystals that turn white when heated. Cu₂SO₄ would be copper(I) sulfate; 6H₂O is hexahydrate; SO₃ is sulfite; Cu(SO₄)₂ needs Cu⁴⁺.",["Build the salt from its ions.","penta = 5.","Write the waters after a centered dot."]],
["m2-a-119",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of Ba(OH)₂·8H₂O?",["barium dihydroxide octahydrate","barium hydroxide heptahydrate","barium hydroxide octahydrate","barium(II) hydroxide octahydrate","barium hydride octahydrate"],2,"Ba²⁺ with two OH⁻ = barium hydroxide (no prefix for the two hydroxides); 8 waters = octahydrate (Silberberg Sample Problem 2.10). Hepta is 7; Ba needs no numeral; hydride is H⁻.",["Ionic part first: Ba²⁺ and OH⁻.","No numerical prefixes for the ions in an ionic name.","octa = 8."]],
["m2-a-120",2,3,4,"Calculation","mcq","What is the formula mass of copper(II) sulfate pentahydrate?",["159.61 amu","177.63 amu","313.24 amu","233.69 amu","249.69 amu"],4,"CuSO₄·5H₂O: Cu 63.55 + S 32.06 + 4 O (64.00) = 159.61 for CuSO₄; 5 H₂O = 5 × 18.016 = 90.08. Total = 249.69 amu. Distractors: 159.61 leaves out the water; 177.63 adds only one H₂O; 313.24 uses two Cu (copper(I)); 233.69 uses sulfite.",["Write the formula from the name first.","The ·5H₂O adds five whole water molecules.","H₂O = 18.016 amu."]],
["m2-a-121",2,3,1,"Worksheet","mcq","What is the systematic name of N₂O₄ (Week 3 worksheet compound)?",["nitrogen tetroxide","dinitrogen tetroxide","nitrogen(IV) oxide","dinitrogen oxide","nitrogen dioxide"],1,"N₂O₄ contains only nonmetals, so it is named as a binary covalent compound with Greek prefixes: di- for 2 N, tetra- for 4 O, with the final a dropped before \"oxide\": dinitrogen tetroxide. The first element takes a prefix whenever there is more than one of it. Roman numerals are for ionic compounds; nitrogen dioxide is NO₂.",["Both elements are nonmetals - use prefixes.","di = 2, tetra = 4.","tetra + oxide → tetroxide."]],
["m2-a-122",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of PCl₅?",["monophosphorus pentachloride","phosphorus chloride","potassium pentachloride","phosphorus pentachloride","phosphorus tetrachloride"],3,"Binary covalent: the first element keeps its name (mono- is NOT used on the first element), the second gets a prefix and -ide: penta = 5 → phosphorus pentachloride. P is phosphorus (K is potassium); tetra = 4.",["Two nonmetals → prefixes.","Drop mono- on the first element.","penta = 5."]],
["m2-a-123",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of carbon disulfide?",["C₂S","CS","C₂S₂","CaS₂","CS₂"],4,"No prefix on carbon means one C; di- on sulfide means two S: CS₂. C₂S puts the 2 on the wrong element; CS ignores the prefix; Ca is calcium.",["The prefix belongs to the element it is attached to.","di = 2, on sulfur.","One C, two S."]],
["m2-a-124",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of SF₄?",["monosulfur tetrafluoride","sulfur pentafluoride","sulfur tetrafluoride","sulfide tetrafluoride","tetrafluorine sulfide"],2,"Two nonmetals: sulfur (first element, no mono-) + tetra (4) + fluoride → sulfur tetrafluoride (Silberberg Sample Problem 2.14 fixes \"monosulfur pentafluoride\"). The element named first keeps its element name; the order follows the formula.",["No mono- on the first element.","tetra = 4.","First element name, then prefix + root + -ide."]],
["m2-a-125",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of dichlorine heptoxide?",["Cl₂O₇","Cl₂O₆","ClO₇","Cl₇O₂","Cl₂O₅"],0,"di = 2 Cl, hepta = 7 O → Cl₂O₇. hexa = 6, penta = 5. Cl₇O₂ swaps the subscripts.",["di- goes with chlorine, hepta- with oxygen.","hepta = 7.","Two Cl, seven O."]],
["m2-a-126",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of N₂O₅?",["nitrogen pentoxide","dinitrogen pentoxide","dinitrogen tetroxide","pentanitrogen dioxide","nitrogen dioxide"],1,"Two N (di-) and five O (penta-, with the a dropped before oxide): dinitrogen pentoxide. Leaving off di- loses information; tetroxide is 4 O (N₂O₄); the last two have the numbers or elements wrong.",["Both elements need prefixes here.","penta = 5.","pent(a) + oxide → pentoxide."]],
["m2-a-127",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of nitrogen monoxide?",["N₂O","NO₂","N₂O₂","NO","N₂O₄"],3,"No prefix on nitrogen = one N; mono- on oxide = one O: NO. N₂O is dinitrogen monoxide; NO₂ is nitrogen dioxide; N₂O₄ is dinitrogen tetroxide.",["mono = 1.","The first element has no prefix, so there is one of it.","One N, one O."]],
["m2-a-128",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","Which name-formula pair for a binary molecular compound is CORRECT?",["SiO₂ - silicon dioxide","SO₃ - sulfite","CO - carbon oxide","N₂O₃ - dinitrotrioxide","SCl₂ - sulfur chloride"],0,"SiO₂ = one Si, two O = silicon dioxide ✓. SO₃ is sulfur trioxide (sulfite is the ION SO₃²⁻); CO is carbon monoxide; N₂O₃ is dinitrogen trioxide (full element name and a space - Silberberg 2.14); SCl₂ is sulfur dichloride.",["Molecular names need prefixes on the second element.","Neutral SO₃ is not the sulfite ion.","One correct pair uses di- correctly."]],
["m2-a-129",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of S₂Cl₂?",["disulfurous dichloride","sulfur chloride","disulfur dichloride","disulfur chloride","dichlorine disulfide"],2,"Two S (di-) and two Cl (di-): disulfur dichloride (Silberberg Follow-up 2.14A corrects \"disulfurous dichloride\"). \"-ous\" is for acids and old ion names; both prefixes are needed; the element written first in the formula is named first.",["Both elements need di-.","Keep the element name \"sulfur\" unchanged.","Name in the order of the formula."]],
["m2-a-130",2,3,4,"Trap","mcq","Which name is CORRECT?",["CaCl₂ - calcium dichloride","CO - carbon monoxide","FeCl₃ - iron trichloride","Na₂O - disodium oxide","CO₂ - monocarbon dioxide"],1,"CO is molecular (two nonmetals), so prefixes are used, and mono- is kept on the SECOND element: carbon monoxide ✓. CaCl₂ and Na₂O are ionic - no prefixes: calcium chloride, sodium oxide. FeCl₃ is ionic with a transition metal: iron(III) chloride. CO₂ is carbon dioxide (no mono- on the first element).",["First decide: ionic (metal + nonmetal) or molecular (nonmetals)?","Prefixes only for molecular compounds.","mono- is dropped only on the first element."]],
["m2-a-131",2,3,1,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of HCl(aq)?",["chloric acid","hydrochlorous acid","chlorous acid","perchloric acid","hydrochloric acid"],4,"A binary acid (H + an -ide anion, chloride) is named hydro- + root + -ic + acid: hydrochloric acid. Chloric (HClO₃), chlorous (HClO₂) and perchloric (HClO₄) acids are oxoacids and take no hydro- prefix.",["The anion is chloride (-ide, no oxygen).","Binary acids: hydro___ic acid.","No oxygen → hydro- prefix."]],
["m2-a-132",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of HNO₂?",["nitric acid","hydronitrous acid","hydronitric acid","nitrous acid","hyponitrous acid"],3,"HNO₂ contains the nitrite ion (NO₂⁻). Oxoacids: -ate → -ic, -ite → -ous, no hydro-. So nitrite → nitrous acid. Nitric acid is HNO₃ (from nitrate).",["Identify the anion: NO₂⁻.","-ite anion → -ous acid.","Oxoacids never take hydro-."]],
["m2-a-133",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of H₂SO₃?",["sulfurous acid","sulfuric acid","hydrosulfuric acid","hydrosulfurous acid","sulfitic acid"],0,"H₂SO₃ = 2 H⁺ + sulfite (SO₃²⁻). -ite → -ous: sulfurous acid (Silberberg keeps the \"ur\" for sulfur acids). Sulfuric acid is H₂SO₄; hydrosulfuric acid is H₂S(aq).",["Anion: SO₃²⁻ = sulfite.","sulfite → sulfurous.","No hydro- for oxoacids."]],
["m2-a-134",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of HClO?",["chlorous acid","hydrochloric acid","hypochlorous acid","perchloric acid","hypochloric acid"],2,"HClO contains hypochlorite, ClO⁻. -ite → -ous and the hypo- prefix stays: hypochlorous acid (Sample Problem 2.12). \"Hypochloric\" wrongly uses -ic for an -ite ion.",["Which chlorine oxoanion has one O?","Hypochlorite.","Keep hypo-, change -ite to -ous."]],
["m2-a-135",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of HIO₃?",["iodous acid","iodic acid","hydroiodic acid","periodic acid","hypoiodous acid"],1,"IO₃⁻ is iodate (like chlorate, ClO₃⁻). -ate → -ic: iodic acid. Iodous acid is HIO₂, periodic acid HIO₄, hypoiodous acid HIO, and hydroiodic acid HI(aq).",["Halogen oxoanions follow the chlorine pattern.","XO₃⁻ is the -ate ion.","-ate → -ic."]],
["m2-a-136",2,3,3,"Concept","mcq","What is the name of HCN(aq)?",["cyanic acid","cyanous acid","hydrocyanous acid","carbonic acid","hydrocyanic acid"],4,"CN⁻ is cyanide, an -ide anion, so the acid is named like a binary acid: hydro- + cyan + -ic + acid = hydrocyanic acid (Sample Problem 2.12). Carbonic acid is H₂CO₃.",["The anion ends in -ide.","-ide anions give hydro___ic acids.","cyanide → hydrocyanic."]],
["m2-a-137",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of phosphoric acid?",["H₃PO₃","HPO₄","H₂PO₄","H₃PO₄","H₃P"],3,"phosphoric (-ic) ← phosphate (PO₄³⁻). Three H⁺ balance 3−: H₃PO₄. H₃PO₃ is phosphorous acid (from phosphite); HPO₄ and H₂PO₄ are not neutral; H₃P has no oxygen.",["-ic acid comes from the -ate ion.","Phosphate = PO₄³⁻.","Add enough H⁺ to make it neutral."]],
["m2-a-138",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the formula of carbonic acid?",["HCO₃","H₂CO₃","H₂CO₂","CH₃COOH","H₂C₂O₄"],1,"carbonic ← carbonate (CO₃²⁻); two H⁺ neutralize 2−: H₂CO₃ (it decomposes to CO₂ + H₂O, the fizz in soda). HCO₃ would still be charged (HCO₃⁻ is hydrogen carbonate); H₂CO₂ has one O too few; CH₃COOH is acetic acid and H₂C₂O₄ is oxalic acid.",["-ic ← -ate.","Carbonate is CO₃²⁻.","Two H⁺ neutralize the 2− charge."]],
["m2-a-139",2,3,4,"Exam","mcq","An acid is formed from the chlorite ion. What are its name and formula?",["chloric acid, HClO₃","hydrochlorous acid, HClO₂","hypochlorous acid, HClO","chlorous acid, HClO₂","chlorous acid, HClO₃"],3,"Chlorite = ClO₂⁻. Adding one H⁺ gives HClO₂. -ite → -ous, no hydro- for oxoacids: chlorous acid. Chloric acid HClO₃ comes from chlorate; \"hydrochlorous\" wrongly adds hydro-; hypochlorous acid HClO comes from hypochlorite; the last choice pairs the right name with the chlorate formula.",["Write the chlorite ion's formula first.","One H⁺ neutralizes a 1− ion.","-ite → -ous."]],
["m2-a-140",2,3,4,"Trap","mcq","Which acid name is INCORRECT?",["HF(aq) - fluoric acid","HBr(aq) - hydrobromic acid","HNO₃ - nitric acid","H₂SO₄ - sulfuric acid","HClO₄ - perchloric acid"],0,"HF contains fluoride (an -ide anion, no oxygen), so it is a binary acid: hydrofluoric acid. The others follow the rules: HBr from bromide → hydrobromic; HNO₃ from nitrate → nitric; H₂SO₄ from sulfate → sulfuric; HClO₄ from perchlorate → perchloric.",["Does the acid contain oxygen?","Binary (no O) acids need the hydro- prefix.","Which binary acid is missing hydro-?"]],
["m2-a-141",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","What is the name and formula of the anion of acetic acid?",["acetite, CH₃COO⁻","acetate, CH₃COO²⁻","acetide, CH₃COOH⁻","carbonate, CO₃²⁻","acetate, CH₃COO⁻"],4,"Acetic acid (CH₃COOH, -ic) loses its acidic H⁺ to give the acetate ion, CH₃COO⁻ (also written C₂H₃O₂⁻). -ic acid ↔ -ate anion; the charge is 1− because only one H⁺ is removed.",["-ic acid ↔ -ate ion.","Acetic acid gives up one H⁺.","One H⁺ removed → 1− charge."]],
["m2-a-142",2,3,1,"Concept","mcq","How many atoms in total are in one formula unit of Ca(OH)₂?",["4","3","5","6","7"],2,"The subscript 2 outside the parentheses doubles everything inside: Ca 1 + O 2 + H 2 = 5 atoms. Trap: 4 counts the H only once; 3 ignores the parentheses entirely.",["A subscript after parentheses multiplies every atom inside.","OH × 2 = 2 O + 2 H.","1 + 2 + 2."]],
["m2-a-143",2,3,2,"Calculation","num","How many atoms in total are in one formula unit of (NH₄)₃PO₄? (Enter a whole number.)",["20"],20,"N: 1 × 3 = 3; H: 4 × 3 = 12; P: 1; O: 4. Total = 3 + 12 + 1 + 4 = 20 atoms. Trap: forgetting to multiply the H by 3 gives 12.",["Multiply everything inside (NH₄) by 3.","Count N, H, P and O separately.","3 + 12 + 1 + 4."],{"abs":0.4}],
["m2-a-144",2,3,2,"Concept","mcq","How many hydrogen atoms are in one formula unit of (NH₄)₂HPO₄?",["5","8","6","9","10"],3,"Two NH₄⁺ ions contribute 2 × 4 = 8 H, and the hydrogen phosphate ion contributes 1 more: 9 H atoms. Distractors: 5 counts NH₄ once; 8 forgets the H in HPO₄; 6 and 10 are miscounts.",["Count H inside the parentheses, times the subscript.","Then add any H elsewhere in the formula.","8 from ammonium plus 1 from HPO₄²⁻."]],
["m2-a-145",2,3,3,"Calculation","num","How many oxygen atoms are in one formula unit of the hydrate Al₂(SO₄)₃·18H₂O? (Enter a whole number.)",["30"],30,"Al₂(SO₄)₃: 4 O × 3 sulfates = 12 O. The 18 waters add 18 × 1 = 18 O. Total = 12 + 18 = 30 O atoms. Trap: forgetting the water of hydration gives 12.",["Count O in the salt and in the waters separately.","SO₄ appears 3 times.","12 + 18."],{"abs":0.4}],
["m2-a-146",2,3,2,"Worksheet","num","Calculate the formula mass of CaCO₃. (Week 3 worksheet, question 1; enter a number in amu.)",["100.09 amu"],100.09,"Formula mass = Ca + C + 3 O = 40.08 + 12.01 + 3(16.00) = 40.08 + 12.01 + 48.00 = 100.09 amu. (The molar mass is the same number in g/mol - the bridge to the mole in the next gym.) Trap: counting only one O gives 68.09 amu.",["Add the atomic masses of every atom in the formula.","There are three O atoms.","40.08 + 12.01 + 48.00."],{"abs":0.02}],
["m2-a-147",2,3,2,"Worksheet","mcq","Calculate the molecular mass of N₂O₄. (Week 3 worksheet, question 2)",["92.02 amu","46.01 amu","78.01 amu","108.02 amu","44.02 amu"],0,"Molecular mass = 2 N + 4 O = 2(14.01) + 4(16.00) = 28.02 + 64.00 = 92.02 amu. Distractors: 46.01 is NO₂ (half the molecule); 78.01 counts N only once; 108.02 is N₂O₅; 44.02 is N₂O.",["Multiply each atomic mass by its subscript.","2 × 14.01 and 4 × 16.00.","Add them."]],
["m2-a-148",2,3,2,"Worksheet","mcq","What is the formula mass of iron(III) oxide, Fe₂O₃?",["71.85 amu","159.70 amu","103.85 amu","127.70 amu","231.55 amu"],1,"2 Fe + 3 O = 2(55.85) + 3(16.00) = 111.70 + 48.00 = 159.70 amu. Distractors: 71.85 is FeO (iron(II) oxide); 103.85 counts Fe once; 127.70 counts O once; 231.55 is Fe₃O₄.",["Fe₂O₃: two Fe, three O.","Fe = 55.85, O = 16.00.","111.70 + 48.00."]],
["m2-a-149",2,3,2,"Worksheet","num","What is the formula mass of lithium sulfite, Li₂SO₃? (Enter a number in amu.)",["93.94 amu"],93.94,"2 Li + S + 3 O = 2(6.94) + 32.06 + 3(16.00) = 13.88 + 32.06 + 48.00 = 93.94 amu. (This is the molar mass the Week 3 worksheet uses to turn 9.234 g into 0.09830 mol.) Trap: using sulfate (SO₄) gives 109.94 amu.",["Two Li, one S, three O.","Li = 6.94, S = 32.06, O = 16.00.","13.88 + 32.06 + 48.00."],{"abs":0.02}],
["m2-a-150",2,3,3,"Calculation","mcq","What is the formula mass of calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)₂?",["57.09 amu","58.10 amu","74.10 amu","114.18 amu","72.08 amu"],2,"Ca(OH)₂ = Ca + 2 O + 2 H = 40.08 + 2(16.00) + 2(1.008) = 40.08 + 32.00 + 2.016 = 74.10 amu. Distractors: 57.09 ignores the subscript 2; 58.10 doubles only the H; 114.18 doubles the Ca too; 72.08 forgets the H.",["The 2 after the parentheses doubles both O and H.","Ca 1, O 2, H 2.","40.08 + 32.00 + 2.016."]],
["m2-a-151",2,3,3,"Calculation","num","What is the formula mass of aluminum sulfate, Al₂(SO₄)₃? (Enter a number in amu.)",["342.14 amu"],342.14,"Al₂(SO₄)₃ = 2 Al + 3 S + 12 O = 2(26.98) + 3(32.06) + 12(16.00) = 53.96 + 96.18 + 192.00 = 342.14 amu. Trap: applying the 3 only to the O (Al₂SO₁₂) or not at all (Al₂SO₄ = 150.02) gives the wrong mass.",["The subscript 3 multiplies S AND O.","Count: 2 Al, 3 S, 12 O.","53.96 + 96.18 + 192.00."],{"abs":0.05}],
["m2-a-152",2,3,3,"Calculation","mcq","What is the formula mass of ammonium phosphate, (NH₄)₃PO₄?",["113.01 amu","131.05 amu","163.11 amu","133.10 amu","149.10 amu"],4,"3 N + 12 H + P + 4 O = 3(14.01) + 12(1.008) + 30.97 + 4(16.00) = 42.03 + 12.096 + 30.97 + 64.00 = 149.10 amu. Distractors: 113.01 ignores the 3; 131.05 uses 2 ammonium ions; 163.11 is the \"(NH₃)₄PO₄\" error; 133.10 uses phosphite.",["Write out the atom counts: N 3, H 12, P 1, O 4.","Multiply each by its atomic mass.","Add the four totals."]],
["m2-a-153",2,3,2,"Calculation","mcq","What is the molecular mass of glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆?",["30.03 amu","168.06 amu","40.11 amu","100.16 amu","180.16 amu"],4,"6 C + 12 H + 6 O = 6(12.01) + 12(1.008) + 6(16.00) = 72.06 + 12.096 + 96.00 = 180.16 amu. Distractors: 30.03 is the empirical formula CH₂O; 168.06 forgets the H; the last two drop subscripts on C or O.",["Multiply each atomic mass by its subscript.","6 C, 12 H, 6 O.","72.06 + 12.096 + 96.00."]],
["m2-a-154",2,3,3,"Calculation","mcq","What is the mass fraction of oxygen in calcium carbonate, CaCO₃?",["0.160","0.400","0.600","0.480","0.120"],3,"Mass fraction of O = (mass of O in one formula unit) ÷ (formula mass) = 3(16.00) ÷ 100.09 = 48.00 ÷ 100.09 = 0.4796 → 0.480. Distractors: 0.160 counts one O; 0.400 is the Ca fraction; 0.600 is the fraction of ATOMS that are O (3 of 5), not of mass; 0.120 is carbon.",["Mass fraction uses masses, not numbers of atoms.","Mass of O = 3 × 16.00.","Divide by the formula mass 100.09."]],
["m2-a-155",2,3,3,"Calculation","num","What mass of calcium is contained in 75.0 g of calcium carbonate, CaCO₃? (Enter a number in g.)",["30.0 g"],30,"Mass fraction of Ca = 40.08 ÷ 100.09 = 0.4004. Mass of Ca = 75.0 g × 0.4004 = 30.03 → 30.0 g. (Silberberg's mass-fraction example: about 40% of calcium carbonate is calcium.) Trap: multiplying by 40.08 directly gives a nonsense mass of thousands of grams.",["Definite composition: Ca is always the same fraction of CaCO₃'s mass.","Fraction = 40.08 / 100.09.","Multiply that fraction by 75.0 g."]],
["m2-a-156",2,3,4,"Calculation","mcq","What is the formula mass of iron(III) nitrate nonahydrate, Fe(NO₃)₃·9H₂O?",["404.02 amu","241.88 amu","259.90 amu","356.02 amu","280.00 amu"],0,"Fe(NO₃)₃ = 55.85 + 3(14.01 + 48.00) = 55.85 + 186.03 = 241.88; 9 H₂O = 9 × 18.016 = 162.144. Total = 404.02 amu. Distractors: 241.88 omits the water; 259.90 adds one water; 356.02 uses nitrite; 280.00 ignores the 3 on nitrate.",["nona = 9 waters.","Nitrate (NO₃) appears three times.","Add the salt and the nine waters."]],
["m2-a-157",2,3,4,"Exam","mcq","What is the molecular mass of dinitrogen pentoxide?",["92.02 amu","108.02 amu","62.01 amu","76.02 amu","94.01 amu"],1,"Name → formula first: di = 2 N, penta = 5 O → N₂O₅. Mass = 2(14.01) + 5(16.00) = 28.02 + 80.00 = 108.02 amu. Distractors: 92.02 is N₂O₄ (tetroxide); 62.01 is NO₃; 76.02 is N₂O₃; 94.01 drops the di- (NO₅).",["Write the formula from the prefixes.","di = 2, penta = 5.","2(14.01) + 5(16.00)."]],
["m2-a-158",2,3,4,"Exam","mcq","What is the formula mass of lithium sulfite?",["109.94 amu","45.94 amu","93.94 amu","87.00 amu","61.94 amu"],2,"Lithium sulfite = Li⁺ and SO₃²⁻ → Li₂SO₃. Mass = 2(6.94) + 32.06 + 3(16.00) = 93.94 amu. Distractors: 109.94 is lithium sulfate; 45.94 is lithium sulfide; 87.00 uses one Li (LiSO₃, not neutral); 61.94 drops two O.",["Get the formula right first: sulfite = SO₃²⁻.","Two Li⁺ balance one sulfite.","Then add the atomic masses."]],
["m2-a-159",2,3,4,"Exam","mcq","What is the formula mass of iron(III) sulfate?",["151.91 amu","207.76 amu","344.03 amu","399.88 amu","351.88 amu"],3,"Iron(III) = Fe³⁺, sulfate = SO₄²⁻ → Fe₂(SO₄)₃ (6+ and 6−). Mass = 2(55.85) + 3(32.06) + 12(16.00) = 111.70 + 96.18 + 192.00 = 399.88 amu. Distractors: 151.91 is iron(II) sulfate; 207.76 reads the numeral as a subscript; 344.03 uses one Fe; 351.88 uses sulfite.",["Formula first: balance Fe³⁺ against SO₄²⁻.","LCM of 3 and 2 is 6.","Fe₂(SO₄)₃: 2 Fe, 3 S, 12 O."]],
["m2-a-160",2,3,4,"Calculation","mcq","What is the formula mass of copper(II) nitrate trihydrate?",["241.62 amu","187.57 amu","205.59 amu","179.61 amu","209.62 amu"],0,"Cu²⁺ + 2 NO₃⁻ → Cu(NO₃)₂ = 63.55 + 2(62.01) = 187.57; tri = 3 H₂O = 54.048. Total = 241.62 amu. Distractors: 187.57 omits the water; 205.59 adds one water; 179.61 uses one nitrate (copper(I)); 209.62 uses nitrite.",["Formula from the name: Cu(NO₃)₂·3H₂O.","NO₃ = 62.01; H₂O = 18.016.","Add salt + 3 waters."]],
["m2-a-161",2,3,4,"Exam","mcq","A molecular oxide of nitrogen has a molecular mass of 76.02 amu. What is its name?",["nitrogen trioxide","dinitrogen tetroxide","dinitrogen trioxide","nitrogen dioxide","dinitrogen monoxide"],2,"Test candidates: N₂O₃ = 2(14.01) + 3(16.00) = 76.02 ✓. NO₃ = 62.01; N₂O₄ = 92.02; NO₂ = 46.01; N₂O = 44.02. So the compound is N₂O₃, dinitrogen trioxide.",["Compute the mass of each candidate formula.","N = 14.01, O = 16.00.","28.02 + 48.00 = 76.02."]],
["m2-a-162",2,3,4,"Exam","mcq","A metal chloride with the formula XCl₃ has a formula mass of 162.20 amu. What is the compound?",["iron(II) chloride","cobalt(III) chloride","chromium(III) chloride","manganese(III) chloride","iron(III) chloride"],4,"Mass of X = 162.20 − 3(35.45) = 162.20 − 106.35 = 55.85 amu → Fe (55.85). With three Cl⁻, iron is Fe³⁺: iron(III) chloride. Check the others: FeCl₂ = 126.75, CoCl₃ = 165.28, CrCl₃ = 158.35, MnCl₃ = 161.29 amu. Trap: iron(II) chloride has the right metal but the wrong formula.",["Subtract the mass of three Cl atoms.","3 × 35.45 = 106.35.","Which metal has an atomic mass of 55.85?"]]
]);
